tools/pipeline/ledger.json:1456:     "note": "SHIP-WITH-FIX. Two findings nobody else had in full: the cap and the domain are INCONSISTENT (the domain admits INT_MAX but cap x base can only ever serve 68,157,440 ticks/s, so above that the debt grows forever and 'delivered by the signals that follow' is untrue), and it is REACHABLE through cpu_mips_coproc at compare_diff==1: 166.56 MHz on Algor, 360 MHz on VoCore. So R4's 'every legitimate case bit-identical' is FALSE. Also found the gate floor at 13 vs 14 rows, the constant-deletion mutant, and interval=0.5/freq sailing over every floor. Sandbox denied execution, so MANUAL not CONFIRMED, and it said so.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:1741:     "note": "SHIP-WITH-FIX. Two findings nobody else had in full: the cap and the domain are INCONSISTENT (the domain admits INT_MAX but cap x base can only ever serve 68,157,440 ticks/s, so above that the debt grows forever and 'delivered by the signals that follow' is untrue), and it is REACHABLE through cpu_mips_coproc at compare_diff==1: 166.56 MHz on Algor, 360 MHz on VoCore. So R4's 'every legitimate case bit-identical' is FALSE. Also found the gate floor at 13 vs 14 rows, the constant-deletion mutant, and interval=0.5/freq sailing over every floor. Sandbox denied execution, so MANUAL not CONFIRMED, and it said so.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:1954:     "note": "SHIP-WITH-FIX. Two findings nobody else had in full: the cap and the domain are INCONSISTENT (the domain admits INT_MAX but cap x base can only ever serve 68,157,440 ticks/s, so above that the debt grows forever and 'delivered by the signals that follow' is untrue), and it is REACHABLE through cpu_mips_coproc at compare_diff==1: 166.56 MHz on Algor, 360 MHz on VoCore. So R4's 'every legitimate case bit-identical' is FALSE. Also found the gate floor at 13 vs 14 rows, the constant-deletion mutant, and interval=0.5/freq sailing over every floor. Sandbox denied execution, so MANUAL not CONFIRMED, and it said so.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:2319:     "note": "HOLD, and it was RIGHT to hold -- this is the seat that caught the granule. It produced the 1 KB counterexample (a four-byte read at 0x13fe crosses a translation boundary but not a 4 KB one) with the three tree citations that make it real: memory_arm.c's non-XScale descriptor type 3, memory_sh.c's SH4_PTEL_SZ_1K, and the VR41xx path. Also found that splitting broke #244's whole-buffer zero promise, and that `offset + len` can wrap. All three fixed. Notably it ANSWERED NORMALLY on this brief after being content-flagged on the pass-1 brief two hours earlier -- same subject, neutral bounds-correctness wording.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:2557:     "note": "HOLD, and it was RIGHT to hold -- this is the seat that caught the granule. It produced the 1 KB counterexample (a four-byte read at 0x13fe crosses a translation boundary but not a 4 KB one) with the three tree citations that make it real: memory_arm.c's non-XScale descriptor type 3, memory_sh.c's SH4_PTEL_SZ_1K, and the VR41xx path. Also found that splitting broke #244's whole-buffer zero promise, and that `offset + len` can wrap. All three fixed. Notably it ANSWERED NORMALLY on this brief after being content-flagged on the pass-1 brief two hours earlier -- same subject, neutral bounds-correctness wording.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:3736:   "state_note": "*** EVIDENCE 2026-08-27: THE TITLE'S PREMISE IS MIS-STATED AND THE UNDERLYING DEFECT IS REAL AND WORSE THAN EITHER THE TITLE OR THE SEAT DESCRIBED. It is a MISSING `else`. ***\n\nThe title blames *wrap-around*. A farmed seat checked that premise -- as the brief asked -- and refuted it: ordinary periodic wrap is handled. **But it confirmed the mechanism, and reading the code shows a sharper defect than the one it named.**\n\n`src/cpus/cpu_mips_coproc.c:796-819`:\n\n```c\nint32_t compare_diff = tmp - cp->reg[COP0_COMPARE];\ndouble hz;\n\nif (compare_diff < 0)\n        hz = tmp - cp->reg[COP0_COUNT];        /* (A) */\n\nif (compare_diff == 0)                         /* SEPARATE statement */\n        hz = 0;                                /* (B) */\nelse\n        hz = (double)emulated_hz / (double)compare_diff;   /* (C) */\n```\n\n**(A) IS AN UNCONDITIONALLY DEAD STORE.** The `if/else` at (B)/(C) is a separate statement, and both its arms assign `hz` -- so whatever (A) wrote is always overwritten. When `compare_diff < 0`, (C) runs and yields a **NEGATIVE** `hz`; `if (hz > 0)` at `:812` is then false and **the timer is neither created nor updated**.\n\n**The three cases, exhaustively:**\n  * `compare_diff > 0` -- (C) gives a positive rate; timer set. **The only working path.**\n  * `compare_diff == 0` -- (B) gives 0; `hz > 0` false; nothing set.\n  * `compare_diff < 0` -- (A) computed the intended value and (C) throws it away for a negative one; `hz > 0` false; nothing set.\n\n**Consequence, which is what the title was reaching for:** if the timer was never created it stays NULL, so **no timer interrupt is ever delivered again**; if it already existed it keeps its stale rate. Either way a guest write that ought to reprogram the timer silently does nothing.\n\n*** THE DEAD STORE IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING, because it shows INTENT. *** The author wrote handling for `compare_diff < 0` and it has never executed. This is not a design choice being second-guessed -- it is a chain of `if`s that lost its `else`, and (A) is the evidence.\n\n**REACHABILITY -- PARTLY SETTLED, and the unsettled half is recorded as unsettled rather than asserted.** The gate is `emulated_hz > 0` at `:797`; **21 assignments to `emulated_hz` exist across the tree.**\n  * **arc: DORMANT, verified here** -- `machine_arc.c` contains ZERO `emulated_hz` assignments, so the gate cannot pass.\n  * **pmax: OPEN.** It DOES set `emulated_hz` (`machine_pmax.c:96`, `:160`), so the `:797` gate passes. The seat argued pmax is nonetheless dormant because its default R3000 is `EXC3K`, citing `cpu_dyntrans.c` -- **a file not read here, so this is recorded as the seat's claim, not as verified.** Settle it before ranking this row.\n  * the seat named `algor` (RM5200/EXC4K, `machine_algor.c:49`) as directly reachable. Also unverified here.\n\n**Observable for a detector:** guest-visible timer interrupt delivery -- `Cause.IP7`. Broken: no assertion at all after a write with `compare_diff <= 0`, or delivery stuck at the stale cadence. Fixed: the newly programmed cadence.\n\n**Related:** see `dfreq`, whose closure text also concerns the MIPS compare write converting a distance-to-deadline into a frequency. **Check for overlap before opening a round on either** -- a queue id covering two defects is how one gets buried when the other closes.\n\n",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:4437:     "note": "*** VERDICT: THE DEFECT IS REAL, BUT THE CITED WITNESS DOES NOT WITNESS IT -- AND THE ROW IS INTERNALLY CONFLATED. DOCUMENTED-ONLY/CLOSE under the stopping rule. *** EVERY ELEMENT RE-VERIFIED HERE BEFORE IT WAS ACTED ON, because this answer was used to correct the carrier: the row's `witness` field is None; its closure names 'the MIPS compare write ... converts the distance to a deadline into a frequency'; the row mentions ARM Footbridge four times and MIPS CP0 zero times; and `footbridge_sites_probe.py:111` execs `-E cats`, an ARM/SA110 machine that executes no MIPS instruction and cannot reach CP0 Compare. That probe returned entirely CORRECT values (vendor 0x1011; 0x00345678 after storing 0x12345678 -- the missing byte is the Footbridge's deliberate 24-bit mask signature, not an error). Its cited `dev_footbridge.c:548` is stale: :548 is `console_putchar` and the timer mask is at :640. **So a witness WAS built, and it witnesses a different path from the one this row's closure defines.** THE MIPS MECHANISM IS SOURCE-DEMONSTRABLE AND WORSE THAN 'a different internal representation': a Compare write installs a PERIODIC host timer (`cpu_mips_coproc.c:796`), every expiry increments `compare_interrupts_pending` (:418), and a guest Compare write acknowledges only ONE pending event while clearing Cause.IP7 (:822). If two or more callbacks have accumulated, the next dyntrans update re-asserts IP7 before any new Count==Compare event -- **a wrong guest-readable Cause value and an extra interrupt exception**, not an observationally equivalent model. IT ALSO KEPT THE EVIDENCE LADDER HONEST: the ~615,384-callbacks-per-signal figure came from a timer-core SCRATCH probe, not an end-to-end MIPS guest observation; and the 40.2-second guest clock freeze that was measured belongs to the separate `fbpending` overflow defect, whose #442 bound is in today's source. Neither witnesses MIPS `dfreq`. *** CONSEQUENCE ACTED ON: CLAUDE.md's line 'dfreq's [no-witness claim] was measured false by building the witness in one session' is a WRONG RECORD and was corrected in both carrier copies. The RULE -- that 'no witness of either kind' is checkable and must be checked -- is untouched and remains one of the most useful lines in the file. ***"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:6470:     "note": "Two seats, independently. The domain admits INT_MAX Hz; the cap delivers at most TIMER_MAX_CATCHUP * TIMER_BASE_FREQUENCY = 68,157,440 ticks/s. Between them the debt grows without bound -- MEASURED at 0.9683 s behind after one simulated second, delivered ratio 0.0317. Reached via cpu_mips_coproc at compare_diff==1 (166.56 MHz Algor, 360 MHz VoCore), so R4's 'every legitimate case bit-identical' is FALSE for those machines. NOT made worse by R4 (the old code spun 33 million iterations per signal at the same rate). The question is whether an unserviceable rate should be rejected, dropped, or left to lag -- a policy decision, which is why it is filed rather than fixed.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:11654:     "note": "FINAL DIFF, FIRED AFTER PASS 2 TO CLOSE THE ROSTER GAP -- and it found what two panels and three adjudications had not. **VERDICT: SHIP THE CODE; the CONTRACT carries a third non-sequitur and it is load-bearing.** Read-only by instruction (a table was mid-run); it said so plainly and named the four mutants it would want instead of guessing. *** THE FINDING: MY CONTRACT WARRANTED FLAG-PAIRING SURVIVAL ON 'every row starts a FRESH emulator ... proving per-instance storage needs TWO controller instances, which this rig does not have'. THAT EXPLAINS THE PROCESS-GLOBAL CASE ONLY. *** Pairing survived because **NO ROW FIRES TWO DIFFERENT SITES IN ONE SESSION** -- a property of the ROW SET, not the rig, and fixable in three lines. It checked all 22 rows to confirm zero did (datawr x2 writes wd_data so the width check is never reached; overrun x2 uses legal 8-bit writes; both value rows seed and read 8-bit). **AN UNMEASURABLE CLAIM WAS HIDING A MEASURABLE ONE BEHIND IT** -- and the measurable half is the round's HEADLINE design decision. Shipped as `wr indep`. *** AND THE PER-INSTANCE HALF IS NOT IMPOSSIBLE EITHER -- 'this rig does not have' is true of landisk and FALSE OF THE FORK: *** `dev_pcic.c:234-242` adds TWO wdc instances unconditionally (0x14000180 and 0x140001f0; its own comment reads 'Yuck. Now there are two.'), reached from every hpcmips subtype. Verified. So that is COVERAGE DEBT, not an impossibility, and the contract now says which. *** A SIBLING-PARITY GAP IN THE CARRIER'S EXACT SENSE. *** `w16 reset` covers reset-on-normal-operation at :607 **BY ACCIDENT** -- it happens to place a legal 8-bit write between its two wide writes. Its mirror at :1025 had nothing: r16 x2 and r32 x2 fire back-to-back, r16 value seeds with a WRITE and checks values not counts, accept r8 is a separate session. So `d->warned_nonbyte_read = 0;` on a legal 8-bit read scored **22/22**. Same predicate, same message modulo WRITE/READ, added in the same hunk pair. Shipped as `r16 reset`. *** A SECOND ESCAPE FROM THE GUARD-FIELD/BODY-FIELD CHECK, VERIFIED BY READING: *** :583's guard reads `warned_unimpl_cmd` (:655) while its BODY reads `d->seccnt` (:660), a field the guest writes directly at :1003 and nothing else touches. So `if (!d->seccnt) { d->seccnt = 1; ... }` there scores a clean sweep, and no row builds a state where the two fields disagree. FILED. *** TWO CONTRACT BULLETS WERE UNSCOPED -- listed under DESIGNED TO CATCH while each was measured at exactly ONE site of six. *** Reset-on-normal was pinned only at :583; guest-resettable-operand only at :607. **A capability claimed generally and measured once is the overclaim the contract exists to prevent.** Both now name their pinned sites and their unpinned ones. *** THE SUBTLEST CATCH: A WRONG CITATION THAT LOOKS RIGHT. *** `dev_wdc.c:89` cited the shared #337 flag as spanning ':709 vs its else'. The round uses PRE-FIX numbering throughout and that convention is otherwise applied correctly -- the seat checked HEAD and withdrew its own wrong-citation finding on the others. But :89 is the ONE citation that ships INSIDE the file it points into, where a reader resolves it against the POST-FIX copy -- and post-fix :709 is `odata = (odata << 8) + wdc_get_inbuf(d);`, an inbuf line inside an endianness conditional, **which reads like a confirmation**. Replaced with a positional reference. IT ALSO STRENGTHENED THE :145 ARGUMENT RATHER THAN WEAKENING IT: the announcement was never a corruption detector to begin with, because the FAST BULK PATH laps the ring by direct pointer arithmetic without ever calling `wdc_addtoinbuf`, so it never announced there at all. That is a better warrant than 'the latch bounds the announcement'. AND IT RE-DERIVED TWO ARITHMETIC CLAIMS INDEPENDENTLY: 1026 x 512 = 525,312 = exactly 2 laps of WDC_INBUF_SIZE; and '500 iterations -> 2000 lines' falls out of the file, because a 32-bit read of wd_data calls `wdc_get_inbuf()` FOUR times. The round never stated that derivation; it corroborates the measurement from the source side. CENSUS CONFIRMED AGAINST THE FILE: exactly ten `fatal(\"` call sites, four #337's and six this round's; the two `printf()` at :391/:437 are inside `#if 0` and correctly excluded. Both gate pins verified by grep (10 and 12). All six guards wrap the announcement ONLY -- checked line by line. No latch is guest-clearable: `memset(d, 0, ...)` occurs once, in DEVINIT."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:11744:   "state_note": "*** STATUS FIRST, BECAUSE THIS NOTE OPENED WITH PRE-FIX GUIDANCE THAT THE SHIPPED FIX HAS ALREADY CONSUMED.  Added 2026-08-29. ***  **FIXED at HEAD** -- #464 (six unlatched guest-drivable `fatal()` sites, re-scoped from one arm to six), #485 (the completion interrupt, without which the abort was polling-only), #486 (repaired the round's own non-discriminating clause-(b) gate row).  A rung-3 witness with both admissibility controls is on the row.  **It stays `in work` for exactly two reasons: a minimax pass-2 seat failure owed a re-fire with a repaired brief, and closure adjudication.**  The title is the FOUNDING claim, which is this ledger's convention and not a wrong record -- but a reader meeting `in work` plus a '100 commands, 100 host log lines' title plus work-it-this-way guidance concludes the flood is live, which is the es438 / [[ledgerstatelies]] shape.\n\n  *** THE THREE-MEMBERED OPTION SET APPLIES DIRECTLY TO THIS ROW (carrier rule, minted 2026-08-25 from #463). *** #463 de-fatalised a device by framing the choice as KEEP the unlatched `fatal()` versus DELETE it, chose delete, and its own research stage then found the third member had been shipping in-tree the whole time: **`memory_rw.c:495-523` bounds a guest-drivable diagnostic to 8 emissions via `n_trunc_msgs`, because 'probing at the end of a device's mapped range is common and benign'.** The carrier already said 'a guest-drivable fatal() needs a latch and not a verbosity flag' -- standing rule, never consulted by that round's two-membered framing. **So before this row is worked: the option set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+BOUNDED-ANNOUNCE, and the first step is to grep the fork for the existing idiom rather than reason from first principles.** #463 also established that a bounded announce is not merely acceptable but RECOVERS the self-announcing property that de-fatalising destroys -- which for an unlatched flood is the whole point, since the flood and the announcement are the same channel.  *** MEASURED 2026-08-25, AND THE ROW IS AIMED AT THE WRONG SITE. *** The opus5 seat built a `cp -a` copy at /tmp/gxwdc, verified freshness independently of MAKE_RC, and left GXEMUL-SEC untouched (verified by `git status --porcelain`: no src file modified). *** THE FLOOD IS CONFIRMED AND IS WORSE THAN THE TITLE SAYS: *** 20 stores produce 20 `fatal()` lines WITH and WITHOUT `-q` (0 `debug()` lines either way), and the OVER-READ site at 500 iterations produced 2000 lines / 158,449 bytes of host log (`regress/artifacts/wdcflood/witness_20260825/n3.out`, preserved off the volatile session scratchpad this row used to cite). *** CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-26: this sentence said \"the read path\", which points a reader at the non-8-bit READ guard. `n3.out` is 2000x \"reading too much\" -- the RING OVER-READ. Same numbers, wrong site named. The correction had already landed in the round block, the source comment and the gate comment while this field kept the false wording -- which is the corrections-land-in-CHANGELOG-only failure this ledger records having made twice before, now three times. It was found by a mechanical sweep for stale claims across all five records, not by reading. *** *** BUT `:583`, THE SITE THIS ROW NAMES, IS THE MORE GATED OF TWO FLOODS. *** It requires an attached IDE image (`:461-468` early-returns with a silenceable `debug()` otherwise) and yields ONE line per store. The unnamed sibling `wdc_get_inbuf` over-read at `:160` requires NOTHING -- landisk adds wdc unconditionally at machine_landisk.c:90 -- is on the READ path, and yields FOUR lines per single guest instruction (measured: mov.b/mov.w/mov.l x20 give 20/40/80). SIBLING CENSUS: ten `fatal()` in dev_wdc.c, FOUR latched by #337, SIX UNLATCHED -- :145, :160, :583, :607, :704, :1025. **Fixing :583 alone leaves the strictly worse :160 floodable at 4x with no precondition**, which is the project's recorded 'grep for its siblings' failure. SCOPE RULING SOUGHT: the round is the FILE, not the line -- six sites, one design, one detector. *** WHY #337 MISSED IT, AND IT IS EXPLICABLE RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT: *** that round's scope was \"each replaced `exit(1)`\", and `:583` was always a `fatal()`, never an `exit(1)`, so it fell outside the enumeration. *** THE OPTION-SET PREMISE WAS REFUTED, AND IT WAS MINE. *** The three-membered option set rule describes member 3 as \"announces the first N occurrences and then goes quiet\". That describes `memory_rw.c:495-523` -- which is the tree's ONLY counter-style bound, is DOUBLE-GATED so the counter never increments at default verbosity (`&&` short-circuits), is GLOBAL not per-instance, and prints no give-up notice. The house idiom is different and mature: **N=1 PER-INSTANCE LATCH plus an explicit \"(further occurrences suppressed)\" in the message text**, at roughly 34 sites across 11 device files. `dev_wdc.c` itself holds FOUR (`warned_badlen`, `warned_atapi_err`, `warned_atapi_dataout`, struct at :75-77) and is the tree's REFERENCE implementation -- dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:147 says its own latch is \"Modelled on dev_wdc.c's warned_badlen (#337)\". *** AND `fatal()` SHOULD BE KEPT, ON A RECORDED HOUSE WARRANT: *** dev_asc.c:250-257 states that under `-q` verbosity settles at VERBOSITY_ERROR, so a WARNING-level debugmsg would be INVISIBLE to the boot-log hygiene grep and an ERROR-level one would trip #261's break-on-ERROR. The chronology runs one way -- #245 chose verbosity-gating; #265/#269, #337, #460, #461 all chose latch + fatal(). TWO HAZARDS FOR THE DIFF, both from the seat: (1) SKETCH RULE -- DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc) has a common tail at :1019-1030 (`ret:` -> memory_writemax64 -> return 1), so any decline at the ACCESS level must `goto ret`; `wdc_command()` itself is void and called at :1003, so returning inside IT is safe. (2) CLAUSE (d) -- the guard would read the new `warned_*` field while the body reads `d->cur_command`, so the detector owes a state where a SECOND, DIFFERENT unimplemented command arrives after the latch has set; the 0x5a-vs-0xaa control is the seed for that row. OPEN QUESTION THE SEAT DECLINED TO SETTLE, recorded as open rather than guessed: #337 notes its two warned_badlen sites SHARE one flag, while #460's gdrom comment records a MEASURED warrant that per-site bits beat a shared bit when two sites run in one session. For pure flood control a shared latch is strictly better; the tradeoff is information loss, and which matters here is unmeasured. PRIVILEGE, stated exactly: no device-layer check exists and memory_rw dispatches on physical address, so \"unprivileged\" overstates it -- the exact claim is \"any code that can reach the I/O window, with no emulator-side gate\".  *** OWNER SCOPE DECISION 2026-08-25: THE ROUND IS THE WHOLE FILE -- ALL SIX UNLATCHED SITES, ONE DESIGN, ONE DETECTOR. *** Asked to choose between the filed single site, the two measured floods, and the file, the owner took the file. So the round covers :145, :160, :583, :607, :704 and :1025 using dev_wdc.c's own #337 idiom (per-instance latch + \"(further occurrences suppressed)\"), keeping `fatal()` on the dev_asc.c:250-257 warrant and keeping the existing `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT`. The reopening rule permits it: same device, same semantic class, one fix design, one detector, no new oracle. **Only :160 and :583 carry a rung-3 witness today; the other four are joined on the SAME-CLASS clause and each should get a one-store witness in pass 1, per #337's measured partial-file miss.**  *** PASS 1 (panel_20260825_195526): agy SETTLED Q1 WITH AN ARGUMENT NOBODY HAD MADE, AND IT DISSOLVES THE EVIDENCE CONFLICT RATHER THAN PICKING A SIDE. *** The brief posed shared-vs-per-site latching as #337 (shares `warned_badlen` across two sites) versus #460 (measured warrant for per-site bits). **Neither is wrong: #337's two sites are the two arms of ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH.** VERIFIED BY READING: dev_wdc.c:709 is `if (cpu->byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN) {` containing :731, and :739 is its `} else {` containing :761. A CPU does not flip endianness mid-session, so those two are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE AT RUNTIME and sharing a flag loses exactly zero information. #460's warrant governs sites that can BOTH fire, which is all six here. **DESIGN SETTLED: SIX separate per-instance latches** (or one bitfield with six masks), never shared across read/write or overrun/over-read pairs. *** Q2 SETTLED: NO SUPPRESSION NOTICE. *** Census verified independently by agy against all of `src/devices/`: only dev_asc.c (3 sites) and dev_vga.c (1) carry it, and dev_wdc.c's own four #337 sites do not. Adding it to six new sites would make the file internally inconsistent; editing the four existing ones is unjustified churn into working code. *** Q3 ANSWERED, AND IT CARRIES AN IMPLEMENTATION TRAP THE NAIVE FIX WALKS INTO. *** `:160` is a GUEST error and is safe to latch -- real hardware returns open bus on a read past the transfer, and the emulator returns `(uint64_t) -1`. **BUT the `return (uint64_t) -1;` MUST STAY OUTSIDE the latch guard.** Nesting it inside `if (!d->warned_...)` makes the SECOND over-read fall through to `d->inbuf_tail = (d->inbuf_tail + 1) % WDC_INBUF_SIZE;` and CORRUPT THE RING POINTER -- the fix would introduce a defect worse than the flood it removes. Same shape at `:1025`, inside the common tail: guard the `fatal()` only; `data[0] = odata;` and `return 1;` run regardless. *** Q5: agy CAUGHT AN INTERNAL CONTRADICTION IN MY OWN BRIEF. *** The proposed design specifies scalar `warned_*` fields while Q5 demanded a detector catch a per-device-versus-per-opcode mutant -- which would require a 256-bit bitmap (`uint32_t warned_unimpl_cmd[8]`). Resolution: the scalar per-site latch is correct (it is what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines), and **the TRUE clause-(d) mutant is a different one: scope `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch guard at :583.** Then the second unimplemented command silently SUCCEEDS. A detector that counts only host log lines passes it clean. That is a better (d) mutant than the one the brief proposed and it is the round's headline detector obligation. TWO FURTHER EVASIONS from the same seat: nesting the `return -1` at :160 (above), and using `static int warned` instead of a per-instance field, which silences a secondary IDE controller at 0x170 because the primary at 0x1f0 already warned. *** Q7 WITNESS COSTS, one of which is a genuine obstacle: *** `:607` = a 16-bit write to wd_seccnt; `:1025` = a 16-bit read from wd_status; `:704` = a store to wd_data with no active command -- all cheap. **`:145` is NOT cheap: WDC_MAX_SECTORS is 512 and WDC_INBUF_SIZE is `512*(512+1)` = 262,656 bytes (verified by reading dev_wdc.c:47-48), so filling the ring by guest stores costs ~65,664 32-bit stores.** The disk-read fill path at :343 moves 512 bytes per sector and would be far cheaper, but it needs an attached image -- i.e. it needs `landiskdisk` first. ALL THREE of agy's checkable claims were VERIFIED against source rather than credited: the endianness branch, the buffer arithmetic, and that wdc_get_inbuf() is called once per BYTE inside 16/32-bit transfers (:616, :620, :622, :623 little-endian; :627, :629, :630 big-endian), which is exactly the measured 4 lines per 32-bit guest load.  *** FULL PASS-1 PANEL COMPLETE (panel_20260825_195526): 8 SEATS ANSWERED. *** codex 501 KB, grok 19.5 KB, minimax 13.6 KB, agy 13.2 KB, kimi-k3:cloud 10.8 KB (the SUBSTITUTE transport, fired by hand -- the CLI is 403-walled), glm 4.4 KB, deepseek 4.1 KB. The kimi CLI is the only seat failure and it is a transport wall, not a seat outage. *** Q2 IS UNANIMOUS ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT ANSWERED IT: NO SUPPRESSION NOTICE. *** *** Q1 SPLIT 3-1-2, AND THE SPLIT IS DECIDED BY EVIDENCE RATHER THAN COUNT. *** SIX separate latches: agy, grok, kimi. FIVE plus a per-opcode set for :583: deepseek. FOUR (pairing): glm, minimax. **The two pairing seats both cite #337's shared `warned_badlen` as the licence -- and that precedent is measurably not a licence.** agy and grok independently derived that #337's two sites are the two arms of ONE ENDIANNESS BRANCH (:709 `if (cpu->byte_order == EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN)`, :739 its `} else {`), so they are mutually exclusive at runtime and sharing loses nothing. grok put it exactly: *\"two endian copies of one check, not a licence to pair different events.\"* kimi added an independent refutation from THIS ROUND'S OWN WITNESS: the :160 over-read fired with NO DISK ATTACHED, so no overrun could have occurred -- **the two ring halves are causally independent, which is precisely what the pairing assumes they are not.** DESIGN SETTLED: six per-instance flags, wrap ONLY the `fatal()`, leave every guest-visible side effect outside the guard. *** Q3: FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY LOCATE THE REAL DEFECT AT :145, NOT :160 -- see `wdcringambig`, filed. *** glm: *\":145 is an emulator error, do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun.\"* minimax: *\":145 IS a real defect that the latch does not fix -- it corrupts the ring and continues, so a TODO is owed there.\"* kimi located it first and most precisely. grok: *\":145 is the unfixed pckbc/asc wrap, not a twin of the measured :160 flood.\"* Only agy said :160 was the site to worry about, and it was answering the question as the brief framed it. *** Q5 WAS CONFUSED IN THE BRIEF AND THREE SEATS SAID SO. *** grok: *\"Q5's per-opcode (d) mutant contradicts the per-instance design the brief actually proposes -- that mutant is a spec change, not a floor test.\"* minimax and agy said the same. deepseek dissents usefully in the other direction: :583's diagnostic VALUE is the opcode, so a single flag hides distinct guest actions and a 256-bit set would preserve it. **Disposition: per-instance scalar for the fix (it is what stops a guest cycling 0x00..0xff for 256 lines); deepseek's per-opcode proposal is a SPEC IMPROVEMENT and is filed, not folded in.** The real (d) mutants, from three seats: scope `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` INSIDE the latch at :583 (agy -- the second unknown command then silently SUCCEEDS); nest the `return (uint64_t) -1;` inside the guard at :160 (agy -- corrupts inbuf_tail); and **reuse a GUEST-RESETTABLE field such as `d->seccnt` as the latch (glm)** -- the sharpest of the three, because the guard then reads a field the guest controls. *** THE :145 WITNESS IS NOT A ONE-STORE WITNESS, AND codex SUPPLIED THE TWO HONEST ROUTES: *** prime `head = WDC_INBUF_SIZE - 1, tail = 0` in the detector and call the producer once; or, for a real guest-path measurement, attach an image and issue **513 undrained IDENTIFY commands** -- each adds 512 bytes through `wdc_addtoinbuf`, and the final byte of the 513th reaches the 262,656-byte wrap. The second route needs `landiskdisk`. *** AND A DETECTOR RULE FROM codex THAT APPLIES TO EVERY ROW: each site row must FIRE THE SITE TWICE. \"A one-off first warning proves announcement, not suppression.\" *** A row that triggers a site once cannot distinguish a working latch from no latch at all -- which is this project's vacuity floor, stated for the latch class specifically.  *** A WRONG RECORD IN THIS ROW, WRITTEN BY ME AND CAUGHT BY THE ADJUDICATOR. CORRECTED 2026-08-25. *** Earlier in this note minimax is attributed the position that :145 is a real defect the latch does not fix, and is COUNTED AMONG SEATS SAYING DO-NOT-LATCH. **That count is FALSE.** minimax's Q3 says verbatim: **\"So: latch both.\"** Its position is latch-and-mark, which is the disposition this round adopted -- it was recorded as dissenting from a ruling it agreed with. *** THE DON'T-LATCH CAMP IS glm ALONE *** (\"`:145` is an emulator error, do NOT latch without fixing the ring overrun\"), which turns a 2-of-8 reading into 1-of-8. **AND glm's OWN FALLBACK SILENCES HARDER THAN THE LATCH IT OBJECTS TO:** it proposes \"downgrade it to `debug()`\", and `debug()` has the early-out at `core/debugmsg.c:375` -- free-running at `verbose == 0` gives `v < 0`, so it vanishes ENTIRELY in production, while a latched `fatal()` still announces the first occurrence and cannot be silenced by `-q`. The dissent's remedy is strictly worse for the property the dissent defends. **Weigh deepseek's Q5 dissent knowing its ring analysis was the panel's weakest:** its \":145 is a guest error -- the guest wrote more data than the ring can hold\" is factually off. The EMULATOR writes that ring on the guest's behalf; the defect is state representation. *Second time in two days that a seat-count in this project's records was wrong in the direction flattering the writer's own ruling. The tally was never the argument -- but a misquoted seat is a wrong record whichever way it points.*  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25 (fable5, main-loop hat, on 8 of 9 mandated seats -- PROVISIONAL on its face w.r.t. the absent ninth, per that seat's own rule). *** **(1) Q5 UPHELD, two sharpenings.** Scalar per-device flag at :583 this round; deepseek's per-opcode set FILED. Per-opcode-versus-per-device is a SPEC choice, and deepseek's framing (\"the scalar IS the (d) mutant\") is circular -- it assumes per-opcode is the baseline and then calls the alternative a weakening. Clause (d) tests the CHOSEN spec's repair; it does not adjudicate between two acceptable specs. Put per-device in the CONTRACT (clause e) -- \"does not claim per-opcode uniqueness at :583\" -- so a later alternating-opcode observation is OUTSIDE the contract rather than a floor breach. **SHARPENING 1, the best single point of the panel: the two-different-opcodes row SHIPS ANYWAY, with the expectation INVERTED -- ONE LINE TOTAL.** Same stimulus deepseek wanted, opposite oracle. It kills a last-warned-opcode implementation (under which an alternating 0x5a/0xaa guest floods forever) AND pins the per-device spec in one row. **SHARPENING 2: the filed item inherits a TWO-membered framing and must not.** Honest weakening of my own precedent: #337's latched messages at :816 and :855 carry NO VALUE PAYLOAD -- :583 is the only site whose message names an opcode -- so \"three latches in this file\" is weaker precedent for discarding value-resolution than it looks. The filed option set is THREE-membered: scalar (shipped), per-opcode set (deepseek), and the in-tree COUNT-BOUND idiom (`n_trunc_msgs`, `src/cpus/memory_rw.c:495-523`) -- which this row already banners and which nobody in the dispute named. **(2) `wdcringambig` DOES NOT BLOCK.** The latch bounds the announcement to first occurrence; it does not silence it. **The corrupting store is ALREADY SILENT TODAY** -- :141-146 stores, advances, then tests, so the wrap line fires once per 262,656-byte lap and the N+1th store that destroys data prints nothing. What the latch removes is repetition of a late, miscorrelated warning. THREE OBLIGATIONS make it honest, all in-round and cheap: (a) /* #NNN */ comments at :145 and :160 naming `wdcringambig` -- \"latch bounds the announcement; the ring invariant is untouched\"; (b) the detector CONTRACT disclaims ring health, and the :145 primed-state row is marked for re-pin under the oracle-correction rule when `wdcringambig` lands; (c) file glm's dissent VERBATIM into `wdcringambig` as provenance. **THE TEMPTING THIRD PATH WAS CHECKED AND REJECTED FOR A MEASURED REASON.** grok and kimi both prefer restructuring :145 to the pckbc #288 shape (check-before-store, drop, latch -- verified in-tree at `dev_pckbc.c:158-167`). It must NOT fold in, and not merely by the reopening rule: **the restructure makes head==tail <=> empty sound ONLY on the byte path.** The bulk producer at :343-349 compares only against WDC_INBUF_SIZE and can land head exactly on tail with NO WARNING AT ALL (:876-879 mirrors it on tail). *pckbc had ONE producer; wdc has THREE.* A byte-path-only repair ships a half-invariant under a record that reads as soundness. **(3) SEQUENCING: `landiskdisk` SPLITS IN TWO.** The PROBE-level -d attach is part of `wdcflood`'s own witness battery and blocks nothing -- verified: :583 is unreachable without a disk (:461-468 early-returns), so the opus flood measurement necessarily ran disk-attached and the route is already proven. The BATTERY-RIG integration -- the half carrying the 28-34% NINSTRS poison -- is an independent deliverable and NOT a blocker. **The primed-head/tail row is a RUNG-1 DETECTOR row and cannot discharge :145's witness**; the 513-IDENTIFY route can, and was verified sound: no ring reset exists at command start (the only head/tail writes are :143, :165, :347-349, :876-879), IDENTIFY feeds wdc_addtoinbuf 512 bytes per command (:525-533), and 513 x 512 = 262,656 = WDC_INBUF_SIZE exactly. **(4) THE REPETITION RULE: DO NOT MINT, and my dichotomy was wrong on BOTH horns.** Clause (a) does NOT cover it -- (a) is DETECTOR-level and the defect is ROW-level: a one-off row at a latched site prints one line pre-fix and one line post-fix, colourless in both worlds, so it contributes nothing to (a) and hides behind rows that do. **But no new clause is needed, because clause (d) already forces it mechanically:** the fix adds six latch predicates, (d)-DEPENDS-ON mandates a null-repair mutant per predicate (flag never set), and the only row shape that reddens a nulled latch is one firing the same site twice with an EXACT count. Codex's sentence is the row-construction corollary of (d) for the latch class -- the same relation the accept-row failability rule bears to the de-escalation clause. Two overlapping statements of one rule is what the carrier refuses by name. **Disposition: one derivation sentence in the round block, not a carrier clause.** If a future latch round ships a SURVIVING one-off row, that measured escape is the (f)-style warrant to mint. **REFINEMENT ON THE ROWS (minimax): repetition with a \"<= 1 line\" oracle passes a mutant that prints ZERO. The oracle must be EXACTLY ONE, THEN ZERO** -- announcement AND suppression, which is the pair a latch actually promises.  *** PASS 2 (panel_20260825_204943) FOUND THREE ESCAPES AGAINST A DETECTOR MEASURED 14/14, AND ONE OF THEM IS A STRUCTURAL BLINDNESS OF THE ROWS RATHER THAN A GAP IN THEM. *** All three are L1 -- pre-ship construction, fixed in-round, not filed. **(1) glm -- THE RESET-ON-NORMAL-OPERATION ESCAPE, and it is the sharpest.** ONE line, reusing the existing flag, guard and branch, added inside an existing case for a VALID command: `d->warned_unimpl_cmd = 0;`. **Every site row fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION with no normal device operation between**, so the flag is never reset between fires and the row stays green -- while a real guest that probes an unimplemented opcode, issues IDENTIFY, probes again, issues IDENTIFY, floods forever. The same one-line trick applies at every one of the six sites. *This is the `#463` distinctness-invariance finding wearing new clothes: rows that test a property back-to-back are invariant under any edit that only changes what happens BETWEEN the two fires.* **(2) agy -- THE COVERAGE CENSUS, CONFIRMED AGAINST MY OWN PROBE.** \"The detector's six site rows do not test six distinct C sites\": `:145` has **ZERO** rows while its sibling `:160` has two, and `:583` has three. Also **the non-byte rows probe 16-bit ONLY** -- `mov.w` at both :607 and :1025, never `mov.l`, so a `len != 1` guard narrowed to `len == 2` would escape. **(3) deepseek -- `d->warned_X = !d->warned_X;`**, claimed to toggle so that fires 1 and 3 print while the detector only fires twice. **READING SAYS THIS DOES NOT WORK** -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard (`if (!d->warned_unimpl_cmd) { d->warned_unimpl_cmd = 1; ...}`), so fire 2 never enters and the toggle never runs. **RECORDED AS A PREDICTION TO BE MEASURED, NOT AS REFUTED** -- this project settles disagreements by measurement, and the seat may be modelling a placement I have not considered. It goes in the second table either way, because the row that would kill it kills a whole CLASS. *** THE ROWS OWED, AND THE THIRD IS THE GENERAL ONE: *** `interleave` -- fire the site, issue a VALID command (0xEC IDENTIFY), fire again, demand ONE line; this is what kills glm's escape. `w32`/`r32` -- 32-bit non-byte access at :607 and :1025, closing agy's width gap. **`fire3` -- fire ONE site THREE times and demand exactly one line**, which kills the toggle class and any period-N latch at once, and is the cheapest row of the three. `:145` remains DISCLOSED-NOT-COVERED in the contract; agy's point stands but the contract already says so. *** AND AN HONEST RECORD ABOUT PROCESS: THE CARRIER'S PRE-TABLE CHECK WOULD HAVE CAUGHT agy's FINDING AND I DID NOT RUN IT. *** Its FIRST item is SIBLING PARITY -- *for each arm the diff touches, compare its observable channels against its nearest sibling arm; an asymmetry is a row you owe*. `:145` with zero rows beside `:160` with two IS that asymmetry, visible by counting, before any mutant was built. The check exists precisely because a table re-run is expensive, and this round is now paying that cost: the first table was measured against a 14-row detector and adding rows changes other mutants' scores, so a SECOND table is owed. **The check costs three greps and about thirty minutes; skipping it cost a table.**  *** THE FULL PASS-2 PANEL (8 of 8 answering; the kimi CLI walled and COVERED BY ITS SUBSTITUTE -- panel.sh reported that correctly for the first time) FOUND AN L2 FLOOR BREACH, TWO WRONG RECORDS IN SHIPPED TEXT, AND A DESIGN CLAIM WITH NO ROW BEHIND IT. *** **(L2 -- FLOOR BREACH, clause (b), grok) `:145` HAS NO DETECTOR ROW, SO THE FIX HAS SIX FIXED SITES AND THE DETECTOR SEES FIVE.** Clause (b) requires failure on a verbatim reinstatement at EVERY fixed site; reinstating `:145`'s unlatched form escapes clean. grok's verdict was DO-NOT-SHIP-AS-A-SIX-SITE-FLOOR and it is right. **Confirmed by counting my own probe: the six site-x2 rows cover FIVE sites -- `P_OVERRUN` appears only in `disjoint`, which fires an unimplemented command and never touches the ring.** The 8/14 -> 14/14 colour change is five tested sites plus `unimpl 2op`. Fix in-round, however late: either add a `:145` row or narrow the fix to five sites. **(L4 -- WRONG RECORD IN SHIPPED PROBE TEXT, codex and grok independently) THE HEADER CONTRADICTS ITS OWN CONTRACT.** It claims to catch *removal of any of the six per-instance latch guards* while the contract eight lines below excludes `:145`. It catches FIVE unique sites. **(L4 -- `disjoint` PROVES LESS THAN ITS NAME, codex.** It exercises only the unimplemented-command transcript, so it proves THAT stimulus matches one regex -- not that all six patterns map uniquely. The row's note overstates it. *** (DESIGN CLAIM WITH NO ROW, codex -- and this is the one I would have missed for good.) 14/14 DOES NOT ENFORCE SIX INDEPENDENT FLAGS. *** Every site test starts a NEW emulator (15 `session()` calls), so **pairing the read/write width flags, or the two ring flags, would pass every isolated row -- as would a PROCESS-GLOBAL latch**, unless one session exercises two controller instances. The round's headline design decision -- six flags, not four -- is therefore UNMEASURED by its own detector. codex adds the mitigating half, checked: the implementation does use six separately initialised per-instance fields, `DEVINIT(wdc)` zeroes the whole allocation, and none is reset or aliased later. **So the CODE is right and the DETECTOR cannot prove it** -- which under clause (e) must be disclosed, not claimed. **(ESCAPE, codex) An inserted `return 1;` immediately after the non-byte-read `fatal()`** leaves the FIRST 16-bit read's buffer untouched while the second runs normally -- and `r16 x2` checks only the line count. Needs a VALUE oracle, not a count. **(ESCAPE, ollama_kimi) `idata = data[0]` at the write-width site is a FOURTH guest-visible side effect** and has no pin row; my Q2 list named three. *** THE EPOCH-LATCH QUESTION (ollama_kimi and minimax, independently) IS ANSWERED AND THE ANSWER IS NO. *** They asked whether a guest-reachable device reset re-zeroes the `warned_*` flags, which would make the shipped code an epoch latch no row can see. Checked by reading: the only `memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct wdc_data))` is at :1126, immediately after the malloc at :1125 in DEVINIT. `COMMAND_RESET` (:484) and `ATAPI_SOFT_RESET` (:601) exist and neither re-zeroes the struct. **A real question, cheaply settled, and worth recording as a negative result so nobody re-asks it.** **(RECORDS, minimax) MY OWN BRIEF'S ROW ARITHMETIC DID NOT ADD UP:** it enumerated 2 controls + 6 site + 3 accept + 1 disjoint = 12 against a stated 14. The two unstated rows are the side-effect rows `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` -- which are the most interesting rows in the file and the brief left them out of its own census. **(CHALLENGE, minimax) `fatal()` RETURNING IS ASSUMED IN THE :1025 COMMENT, AND \"no early-out\" DOES NOT ESTABLISH IT** -- no-early-out is about verbosity, not program lifetime. The claim is true and this project settled it on 2026-08-21 by reading `core/debugmsg.c:384-390`, but the comment should CITE that rather than lean on the adjacent no-early-out fact. A fair catch. **(USEFUL FOR `wdcringambig`, ollama_kimi) A WASTE-ONE-SLOT DISAMBIGUATOR IS NOT FORBIDDEN BY CAPACITY:** 512 x 512 = 262,144 usable is still <= 262,655, so a max-length sector transfer still fits. Only the 513-undrained-IDENTIFY fill exceeds it. **Any future claim that \"waste-one breaks the exact-fit sizing\" is wrong**, and that is worth carrying to the ring round before it invents the objection.  *** KILL TABLE 1 -- 8 MUTANTS AGAINST THE 14-ROW DETECTOR, AND ONE ESCAPED. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table1_14rows/` (12 files + a README stating what the numbers mean and why the directory is stamped). Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. SCORES, every reddened row re-extracted with an ANCHORED trailing-`FAIL` match: `base` 14/14 (identity -- the harness scores the unmutated copy exactly as SEC does); `Mb1` 12/14 delete the :583 guard -> `unimpl x2`, `unimpl 2op`; `M1` 12/14 null the repair, flag never set -> same two; `M2` 11/14 SET BEFORE ANNOUNCE, zero lines -> those two plus `disjoint`; `M3` 13/14 last-warned-opcode -> **`unimpl 2op` ALONE**; `M4` 13/14 ABRT moved inside the guard -> **`abrt 2nd` ALONE**; `M5` 13/14 `return -1` moved inside the guard -> **`overread -1` ALONE**; `M6` **14/14 -- ESCAPED, ZERO ROWS REDDENED**. *** THE THREE SINGLE-ROW KILLS ARE THE USEFUL RESULT, NOT THE SCORES. *** `M3`, `M4` and `M5` each redden exactly ONE row, and those three rows exist for exactly those escapes -- nothing else in the file covers them. `abrt 2nd` and `overread -1` are the two rows that read a guest-visible SIDE EFFECT rather than a line count, and they are the only rows that can see a repair swallowed by a guard. *** M2 IS THE MEASURED VINDICATION OF AN ORACLE CHOICE. *** It sets the flag before the test so the site emits ZERO lines. **A `<= 1 line` oracle would have passed it**; `exactly 1` catches it. That choice was argued from a seat's reasoning and is now measured. *** AND M6 IS THE ESCAPE, PREDICTED BY A SEAT ON PASS 2 AND CONFIRMED HERE. *** It swaps the latch operand for the GUEST-RESETTABLE `d->seccnt`: `if (len != 1 && !d->seccnt) { d->seccnt = 1; fatal(...); }`. Every row in table 1 fires its site TWICE IN SUCCESSION, so nothing between the fires ever puts the operand back and the mutant looks perfectly latched. A guest that writes `seccnt = 0` between wide writes floods without bound. **This is a STRUCTURAL blindness of the row design, not a missing mutant** -- the same shape as `#463`'s distinctness invariance, where rows that assert a property back-to-back cannot see an edit that changes only what happens BETWEEN the assertions. *** DETECTOR NOW 22 ROWS, MEASURED 22/22 ON THE FIXED BUILD (`table2_22rows/base_22rows.raw`). *** Seven rows added, each closing something table 1 or pass 2 exposed: `overrun x2` (the :145 row -- **this is what closes the L2 floor breach**, and it needed a hardware loop, `dt r3` / `bf` with a computed displacement, because 513 undrained IDENTIFYs unrolled would be 513 debugger commands); `interleave` (fire, VALID command, fire); `w16 reset` (fire, guest CLEARS the operand, fire -- the M6 killer); `fire3` (kills period-N latches, not just period-2); `w32 x2` and `r32 x2` (the width rows probed 16-bit only, so a guard narrowed to `len == 2` escaped); `r16 value` and `w16 value` (value oracles at the two width sites, for a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- which a count-only row cannot see). Row hygiene checked: no name over 13 chars, no duplicates, no name that is a prefix of another followed by a space. TABLE 2 is running against the 22-row detector with three mutants table 1 could not have had: `Mb2` removes the :145 guard verbatim (the clause-(b) reinstatement at the site that had no row), `M7` is the reset-on-normal-operation escape, `M8` is the period-2 latch. All three anchors were verified to occur EXACTLY ONCE in the source first -- **a mutant that fails to apply scores a false clean**, which is the worst possible failure for a kill table. *** A CAPTION DEFECT IN THE APPARATUS, FOUND AND FIXED AT SOURCE. *** `killwdc.sh` wrote its header at start with the row count and the pre-fix score as LITERALS (`14 rows`, `8/14`). The moment pass 2 forced seven more rows, table 2's own header became wrong about its own size. The script now DERIVES the count (`grep -c '^row(' ...`) and stamps a UTC start time instead of quoting scores it cannot know. Table 2's already-written header is stale and is corrected in place; the script fix stops it recurring. *A table that misreports its own size cannot be cited safely at all.*  *** KILL TABLE 2 IS CLEAN: 22-ROW DETECTOR, TEN MUTANTS, **ZERO ESCAPES**, EVERY LOG INTERNALLY CONSISTENT. *** Preserved at `regress/artifacts/wdcflood/table2_22rows/` with a header that DERIVES its row count rather than quoting one. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. base 22/22; Mb1 18/22; M1 18/22; M2 17/22; M3 21/22; M4 21/22; M5 21/22; M6 21/22; M7 21/22; M8 21/22; Mb2 21/22. **Consistency check on all eleven logs: score + anchored-red count == 22 in every one**, which is the check that would catch a contaminated extractor. *** SIX ESCAPES, SIX SINGLE-ROW KILLS, EACH BY THE ROW BUILT FOR IT -- and that one-to-one correspondence is the result, not the scores: *** M3 last-warned-opcode -> `unimpl 2op` ALONE; M4 ABRT inside the guard -> `abrt 2nd` ALONE; M5 `return -1` inside the guard -> `overread -1` ALONE; M6 guest-resettable operand -> `w16 reset` ALONE; M7 reset-on-normal-operation -> `interleave` ALONE; Mb2 the :145 verbatim reinstatement -> `overrun x2` ALONE. **Nothing else in the file sees any of them.** *** THE L2 FLOOR BREACH IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT. *** `Mb2` is the exact edit that swept CLEAN against the 14-row detector -- removing :145's guard verbatim -- and it now reddens `overrun x2`, the row that did not exist when the breach was found. Clause (b) holds at all six fixed sites. *** M8 VINDICATES A CAUTION, AND THE CAUTION IS THE TRANSFERABLE PART. *** deepseek proposed `d->warned_X = !d->warned_X;` as a period-2 escape. I read its SPECIFIC edit as unworkable -- the assignment sits INSIDE the guard, so fire 2 never reaches it -- and recorded it as **\"a prediction to be MEASURED, not refuted\"**. M8 constructs the same class differently (toggle BEFORE the guard) and escapes a two-fire oracle exactly as deepseek said the class would. **Wrong about the placement, RIGHT ABOUT THE CLASS.** Declaring it refuted on the strength of a correct reading of one construction would have lost a real escape. *A seat can be wrong about the mechanism and right about the family; the family is the part worth building a row for.* *** CLAUSE (c), THE NULL MEDIUM, IS NOW MEASURED AND IN ITS STRONGEST FORM: 0/22 ON BOTH. *** `nullmedium_absent.raw` (a binary that does not exist) and `nullmedium_notanemu.raw` (a real executable that is not an emulator) each score **0/22 -- every row fails, not merely some**. It had NOT been verified before; the question was raised against the round's own readiness and answered in seconds. **AND THE MEASUREMENT EXPOSED A DESIGN PROPERTY THAT WAS ACCIDENTAL RATHER THAN INTENDED:** the three accept rows expect ZERO diagnostics, so a naive implementation would PASS against silence -- zero is exactly what silence produces. They fail correctly only because `n()` returns `\"died\"` rather than `\"0\"` when the run produced nothing (probe :227-228). **That single line is what stops every accept row in this file from being vacuous under clause (c)**, and it was not written for that reason. Worth carrying to any future detector with absence-shaped oracles: an \"expect 0\" row MUST distinguish 'ran and saw nothing' from 'did not run'.  *** THE READINESS ADJUDICATION RETURNED **HOLD-FOR** AND FOUND TWO SHIPPED ROWS VACUOUS. ASKING BEFORE THE TABLE FINISHED IS WHAT MADE THAT POSSIBLE -- a clean sweep invites ratification. *** **`r16 value` WAS VACUOUS AS BUILT.** Its oracle was `printed(t,\"r2\") != \"absent\"` against a wanted `\"read\"`, so it passed whenever the debugger echoed ANY hex value -- structurally incapable of failing while the emulator lived. **Confirmed: green under every one of table 2's ten mutants -- it did not exist for table 1, which measured a 14-row detector, and an earlier note said 'tables 1 and 2', which is false.** The `live` control already covered session death, so the row measured nothing at all. **AND BOTH WIDTH VALUE ROWS CHECKED THE WRONG FIRE.** The escape they cite -- a `return` inserted after a latched `fatal()` -- corrupts the FIRST access (flag unset, guard entered, return skips the repair); the SECOND finds the flag set, skips the guard and repairs normally. Both rows read the value ONCE, after both fires, and so saw a perfectly good value. The contract line claiming they carried value oracles \"for exactly that edit\" was FALSE AS BUILT. Repaired to check BOTH fires, with a low-byte mask because the repair sets only `data[0]` and asserting the upper byte would make the row about the wrong thing. *** TABLE 3 EARNS THE REPAIRED ROWS THEIR MEASURED KILL, WHICH IS WHAT A REPAIRED ROW OWES: *** `table3_repaired/`, base 22/22. `Mv1` (delete `idata = data[0]`, the write-side repair) 18/22 -> r16 value, w16 value, overrun x2, accept ec. `Mv2` (delete `data[0] = odata`, the read-side repair inside the `ret:` tail) 18/22 -> devsig, abrt 2nd, r16 value, w16 value. **`Mv3` (codex's EXACT edit -- insert `return 1;` after the latched READ `fatal()`) 21/22 -> `r16 value` ALONE.** *** Mv3 IS THE ONE THAT PROVES THE REPAIR RATHER THAN THE ROW. *** It corrupts only the first access, so **the ORIGINAL one-value form would have scored 22/22 against it** -- the escape would have shipped invisibly behind a row whose whole stated purpose was to catch it. One row in twenty-two sees it, and it is the repaired one. COLLATERAL WORTH READING RATHER THAN DISMISSING: `Mv2` reddens `devsig`, because deleting `data[0] = odata` breaks the status read the device-signature control depends on. That is the control doing its job -- a device that cannot answer through its own decode is exactly what `devsig` exists to notice -- not a spurious red. Tree restored and cmp-verified against SEC, sentinel cleared, zero live processes. All three logs internally consistent (score + anchored reds == 22). *** THE STANDING LESSON, and it is the second time in one round: A REPAIRED ROW IS NOT A ROW UNTIL A MUTANT KILLS IT. *** Both rows looked correct after repair and one of them still could not have caught the edit it was written for. Reading a row and believing it is exactly the state the vacuity floor exists to refuse.\n\n*** codex (rating pass, 2026-09-07) reports this as `low residual -- FIXED`, and I VERIFIED the\nlatch -- but this row is `in work` with its own commit, so I am NOT proposing closure. ***\n\n**Verified:** `dev_wdc.c:1065` is `if (!d->warned_unimpl_cmd) {` around the `fatal()`, with\n`d->error |= WDCE_ABRT;` and `d->int_assert = 1;` deliberately OUTSIDE it at `:1063-1064` -- the\nplacement `#464` (`baacf9b`) and `#485` (`d819188`) established, and the comment at `:1060-1062`\nrecords why.\n\n**Why not closed:** an `in work` row with a commit may carry an outstanding obligation that a\nsource reading cannot see, and this file's sibling row [[wdcperopcode]] is the residual of exactly\nthis fix.  **The closure question goes to the adjudication with the evidence assembled, not to me.**",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18450:     "note": "Pre-existing upstream, untouched by #461, found by the pass-2 measuring seat while building mutants against the alias fix. NOT joined: #461's semantic class is 'unimplemented offset'; this is 'implemented offset, wrong width' -- a different fix design (read-modify-write honouring len, which raises an endianness question the default arm never has to answer) and a different oracle, so the round-scope rule makes it a separate round. *** IT IS THE SAME FAMILY AS THE ALIAS #461 DID FIX, AND #461's NEW RULE DOES NOT COVER IT: *** 'alignment alone separates unimplemented word from misaligned access to an implemented one' is true and says nothing about an ALIGNED NARROW write to an implemented offset. A future reader applying that rule here would conclude it is safe. Related to [hlen], but the OPPOSITE direction: hlen is a wide access spanning registers, this is a narrow access clobbering one. `dma seed` is one store away from being a witness."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18485:     "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Reached codex's discriminating row independently, with the same three expected values including the endianness split on Option A. Its amendment wording is the crisper of the two: 'An aligned offset reaching the default arm is genuinely an unimplemented word. This does not imply that an aligned narrow access to an IMPLEMENTED offset is safe; narrow accesses to implemented registers are a separate width question and are currently not width-discriminated.'"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18492:     "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Option B with the clearest cost/benefit statement: one branch on `len != 4` before the tail store; consistent with #461's decline-rather-than-corrupt philosophy; establishes the missing in-tree precedent for the WIDTH axis without committing to Option A's endianness-dependent merge; and it 'turns an unreachable silent-clobber into an unreachable LOUD no-op'. Explicitly ranked B over C: 'B is C plus a guard for the same engineering cost.'"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18506:     "note": "[panel_20260823_215652] Went furthest on the endianness question the brief called the real technical risk, working out what memory_readmax64 does to a narrow value and where a byte write at +0x04 lands in dma_reg[1]. Its answer is the reason Option A is unsafe to implement from the brief alone: the fact is derivable but was NOT established in the tree, and SH-4 runs either endianness."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:18513:     "note": "*** VERDICT: OPTION C, OVERTURNING A UNANIMOUS 7-OF-7 PANEL FROM SOURCE. *** The panel converged on Option B (decline narrow writes). This seat fetched mainline Linux drivers/cdrom/gdrom.c and read it line by line: :622 `__raw_writeb(1, GDROM_DMA_STATUS_REG)` is the DMA TRIGGER -- a BYTE WRITE to this window's +0x18 on every transfer -- and :618 byte-READS the same offset to poll for completion, while the four value registers at :585-589 are always __raw_writel. *** SO OPTION B WOULD BREAK A BOOTING LINUX, *** and the width-blind code delivers exactly the semantics the one real narrow user needs. The defect's actual content -- clobbering a value register's upper bits -- sits in cells no known guest reaches. It also checked NetBSD (never touches the DMA window) and promemul/dreamcast.c (zero references), and was explicit that KallistiOS widths are BELIEVED only. *** AND IT REFUTED OPTION A ON A DIFFERENT AXIS THAN THE BRIEF EXPECTED. *** The brief called endianness the real technical risk; the seat showed it is DERIVABLE (cpu_sh.c:96 sets EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN UNCONDITIONALLY, so there is no big-endian SH in this tree and the merge is always the low lane -- the brief's 'SH-4 can run either endianness' is true of silicon and FALSE of this tree, verified independently). The real objection is an ABSENT ORACLE: 0x005f74xx is Sega's Holly ASIC, outside the SH7750 manual, and this project holds NO documentation for it -- so the filing's own 'real hardware leaves the upper 24 bits' was an UNSOURCED CLAIM, the #357/#362 shape. A would ship an invented behaviour and pin the invention with a detector row. ON PRECEDENT it corrected the panel again: the BSC16 idiom (dev_sh4.c:1620-1680) is the direct shape-match, but *** IT TRANSFERS BY ITS WARRANT, NOT ITS SHAPE *** -- its warrant is 'a real OpenBSD/landisk boot makes ZERO non-16-bit BSC accesses (measured), so the latch costs a correct guest nothing', and that warrant FAILS here. Decided design recorded as B': decline at the four VALUE registers only, keep +0x18 byte-capable. It also flagged that the row pinning the REAL contract is currently UNBUILDABLE -- any nonzero store at +0x18 hits one of sh4gdromexit's filed exit(1) kills -- so this item is gated behind that round by probe-buildability rather than by ranking."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24446:     "note": "MEASURING seat (Agent tool); ran two read-only emulator launches, otherwise read.  PART (2) IS THE WRONG ONE, and its fbpending half is wrong ON THE MERITS: the probe's own header (:36-45) says the post-fix backlog is a MODULAR RESIDUE of the tick count (3 at 4 s, 100 at 46 s, 375 at 0.5 s -- not monotone in accrual), so `--expect-cap' is one-sided and cannot distinguish 762 from 7; `--learn' is a disassembly dump, not a cap-learner; and THE BOUND'S VALUE IS ALREADY GATED BY AN EQUALITY -- regress/diff_fbpending_bound.c (R5 the bound IS this timer's rate; R6/R6b) run by gate_offline.sh:1259-1277, green.  A cap is strictly weaker than shipped coverage aimed at the same property; the header records a one-token mutant (freq/100.0) that 'destroyed 99% of the guest's clock while passing this probe' under any cap.  Right decision: NO CAP -- report-only mode already ends sys.exit(0 if ok else 1) on ELEVEN control rows, a real failable oracle for APPARATUS ROT, wirable today if named for what it tests.  hpcmips: gate_asan_sweep.sh IS the home (verified: hpcmips is not on AB_EXEMPT so the sweep already requires every subtype to construct; :539 hosts sgi_eaddr_probe.py on build/gxemul with the same shape; 55 s sweep, no wall-clock oracle; -x has precedent at gate_sh_rounding.sh:434) -- BUT the section must sit BEFORE the `gate_skip \"no ASan build\"' at :99, which EXITS the gate (silent coverage loss scored SKIP -- sgi_eaddr already inherits it), and the wiring must name its ADDITIVE rows (C3/C4 acceptance-is-a-real-lookup, S2 source order, R1's fail-closed allowlist) because the headline is already covered by the sweep.  THE FOUR WDC FILES ARE 3 + 1: _sig and _4bit `import wdc_altoffset_probe as W'; addtoinbuf is a separate instrument with a poisoned-image, disk-content-tied, endianness-axis dependency.  *** MEASURED: _sig's 'needs a ciR: CD-ROM rig the battery does not build' is FALSE -- _images/wdc-scratch-64m.img attached `ciR:' reports 'IDE CD-ROM id 0, read-only'; the c is a PREFIX. ***  _4bit's variadic legs already accept one; its single-leg form is a NEW ORACLE choice, not a mode.  SEVEN obligations, not six: gate_offline.sh:1084 SM_EXEMPT fbpending_bound:2026-09-20, enforced at :1165 -- and it is the only genuinely cheap one.  The ledger's EIGHT was correct when written (8 -> 7 -> 6, two discharges on 2026-08-30): an expired measurement, not a transcription.  AND THE POPULATION GROWS THROUGH AN UNDATED DOOR: >= 8 of 18 CLASS_WITNESS entries are post-fix-green (the checker's own definition of a detector), invisible because un-gated witnesses render `not run (correct)'; today's lca_irqstub_probe.py note is an instance.  Three of its own derivers failed and were caught by contradictions between its own outputs -- recorded in its answer."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24456:     "note": "SECOND REVISION, on 7 of 9 seats (opus5 now landed; agy not fired).  THE FIRST REVISION, ONE ENTRY ABOVE, IS SUPERSEDED IN THREE PLACES AND SAYS SO HERE RATHER THAN BEING EDITED: it kept fbpending design-blocked on a cap derivation (the cap is the WRONG INSTRUMENT -- the bound is already gated by an equality); it kept four WDC files in one round (they are 3 + 1, and _sig's media blocker is MEASURED FALSE); and it counted six obligations (there are SEVEN, and my own deriver printed the seventh at check_probe_wiring.py:1328 and I read past it -- the third correlate-not-the-property error of the day, all mine).  Every Opus claim relied on here was re-verified in the tree before this was written: gate_offline.sh:1084/:1165, check_probe_wiring.py:1328, sig_probe.py:75, gate_asan_sweep.sh:99.  DISPOSITION, REV 2.  (1) TWO rounds, not one: `wdcaltoffleg' owns the three altoffset files (one module, one rig, one media family; the parent's `ok' first; _4bit's single-leg form is an ORACLE CHOICE and says so; _sig owes an oracle REDESIGN, not a bolted-on contract); `wdcsingleleg' is RETITLED to addtoinbuf alone, with the poisoned image and the endianness axis as its dependencies.  (2) THREE things are decidable TODAY and all three go in the wiringcliff commit after #516: hpcmips WIRED into gate_asan_sweep.sh BEFORE the :99 gate_skip, additive rows named, its EXEMPT entry DELETED in the same edit; fbpending WIRED with NO CAP as an apparatus-rot row named for what it tests, its EXEMPT entry deleted in the same edit, and the SM_EXEMPT fbpending_bound lane discharged the same way (it is the cheapest item in the set); the checker's FALSE comment at :1333-1334 and _sig's FALSE reason string corrected as L4s.  (3) The three altoffset entries re-dated naming `wdcaltoffleg'; addtoinbuf re-dated naming `wdcsingleleg'; each DELETED in the same edit as its wiring when its round ships.  (4) NEW ROW `witnessclassdoor' (med): the CLASS_WITNESS field is an undated door for un-gated detectors; the fix is a class rule with a dated cutoff, like every checker change here -- its own round, not a rider.  On the record: the ledger's EIGHT was an expired measurement (8 -> 7 -> 6), not a transcription; DERIVED section 9 corrects section 1."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24497:   "note": "*** MY OWN BRIEF'S PREMISE WAS THAT `gdromcondlies` HELD THE LAST LIVE GUEST-REACHABLE HOST KILLS.  IT IS FALSE, AND THE FIRST FILE I SAMPLED REFUTED IT. ***  I stated it AS A QUESTION in that brief (*'have I confused \"the queue knows of no others\" with \"there are no others\"?'*), which is the only reason this was cheap to find -- and I found it myself, before the panel answered.  **THE CENSUS SAID WHERE TO LOOK AND WAS RIGHT.**  `exit_census.py` reports 99 live terminating calls inside `DEVICE_ACCESS` across 30 files; `dev_sgi_re.c` carries **21**, seven times the gdrom file.  It is the top of that list, and I sampled it for exactly that reason.  *** WHAT IS THERE, AND IT IS A CLASS THIS PROJECT HAS ALREADY FIXED ELSEWHERE: ***  the guard at the head of the CRIME_RE_TLB_A arm is  `if (len != 8) { fatal(\"TODO: unimplemented len=%i ...\"); exit(1); }`  -- **a GUEST CHOOSING AN ACCESS WIDTH ends the host process.**  That is the `#337` class verbatim, whose own comment in `dev_wdc.c` reads *'a 3- or 8-byte access to the data port used to call exit(1) -- a GUEST choosing an access width killed the host process'*.  The adjacent arm kills on a plain READ (`fatal(\"TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A\"); exit(1);`), which is the direction/offset class.  **REACHABILITY CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED**: `machine_sgi.c:500` calls `dev_sgi_re_init(machine, mem, 0x15000000)` and the device registers at `baseaddr + 0x1000` through `memory_device_register`.  It is a real device on a real machine, mapped into the guest address space.  **HONEST BOUND ON THE NUMBER, because this project keeps getting counts wrong: 21 is how many live terminating calls sit inside that file's `DEVICE_ACCESS` handlers.**  It is NOT a claim that 21 are individually reachable -- some may be guarded, and `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:1055` is the standing proof that a `DEVICE_ACCESS` site can be unreachable and take a hand-derived warrant to establish it.  What IS established: at least two of them are reachable by construction, and they are of classes already recognised as defects here.  *** THE GENERAL LESSON, AND IT IS ABOUT THE QUEUE RATHER THAN THIS DEVICE: A QUEUE-COMPLETE CLAIM IS NOT A TREE-COMPLETE ONE. ***  The adjudicating seat flagged exactly this limit about its own answer hours earlier -- *'my no-other-live-host-kills claim rests on the queue and this file's grep, not a fresh tree-wide exit census'* -- and then I wrote the queue-complete version into a brief as a premise anyway.  The seat named the gap; the brief inherited it.  SCOPE: this is a CLUSTER and must not become one round.  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, with a second joining only if it is witnessed, in the same device and semantic class, and covered by the same detector with no new oracle.  The width guards may qualify as one class; the read-direction kills are a second.  **More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.**  NEXT STEP is a rung-3 witness on ONE site, not a fix: `-E sgi` machines exist in tree, the device is mapped at a known base, and the `#337` precedent gives the fix shape (latched decline, `(once)` marker) if a witness lands.  *** THE DIAGNOSIS ABOVE IS INCOMPLETE AND THE TRUE ONE IS WORSE.  Corrected the same day by the opus5 seat, which MEASURED it. ***  This row was filed saying the brief had confused 'the queue knows of no others' with 'there are no others'.  **Neither.  The queue knew of NINE others.**  Ledger row `n6` is OPEN, titled *'The record says the footbridge host-halts were made graceful; nine of them are not'*, carrying a CONFIRMED rung-3 witness (`probe:regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py` row K1, gated in `gate_arm.sh`) and a `state_note` RANKING IT 6th OF 8 on 2026-08-27 -- three days before the brief claimed there were none.  The seat rebuilt HEAD clean and re-ran the COMMITTED probe: a guest `ldr r7,[r0,#392]` on an unmodified `-E cats` ends the host at `dev_footbridge.c:589`.  It then swapped the single planted word three more times and got **four of four** -- `:604` ENABLE_CLEAR read, `:565` IRQ_STATUS write, `:712` SDRAM_BA_MASK read -- each one guest instruction, each with the site's own diagnostic text as the attribution control.  **So this is the UNREAD-ROW class, not a census gap.**  The census work still stands and still found two REAL clusters nobody had filed; what it did not do is what a `grep n6` would have done in one second.  *The lesson is not 'census harder'.  It is that a completeness claim must be checked against THE QUEUE'S OWN OPEN ROWS before it is written down -- and that is the cheaper check of the two.*  *** THE CENSUS IS DONE, AND IT SPLITS THE 21 INTO FOUR CLASSES WITH ONE PROVABLY DEAD SITE.  agy seat, 2026-08-30, `regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/witness_20260830/`. ***  It is a READING, not a measurement, and is recorded as one -- but the single most surprising claim in it was CHECKED HERE BY OPENING THE FILE, and it held.  **20 of 21 guest-reachable; 1 PROVABLY UNREACHABLE.**  WIDTH (a guest choosing an access size ends the host) -- 7 reachable: the `len != 8` guards on CRIME_RE_TLB_A/B/C, CRIME_RE_TEX and CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A/B, plus `sgi_de: len = %i not implemented` (which wants len 4 or 8).  DIRECTION (a legal-width access in the wrong direction) -- 6 reachable: `TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A/B/C`, `..._TEX`, `..._LINEAR_A/B`, each reached by an 8-byte READ, i.e. **the guest does everything right except the direction**.  BADOFFSET -- 1: the `unimplemented read/write to address` arm, reached by any access to an unregistered sub-window.  BADVAL -- 6: `sgi_de: UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op`, and five `sgi_mte` arms (MTE_MODE_STIPPLE, unimplemented SRC, MTE_MODE_COPY, MTE_DEPTH_x, and an unimplemented bytemask).  *** THE UNREACHABLE ONE IS DEAD CODE AND MUST NOT BE 'FIXED' -- I VERIFIED THIS BY OPENING THE FILE RATHER THAN ACCEPTING IT. ***  `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]` sits in the trailing `else` of `if (len == 4) { ... } else if (len != 4) { ... } else { fatal; exit(1); }`.  For every integer `len` one of the first two arms is taken, so the third can never execute.  A round that 'de-fatalises' it would be editing unreachable code and its detector could carry no row -- **the vacuous shape in its purest form, and the census is what stops it being attempted.**  **WHAT THIS MEANS FOR SCOPE, and it is the useful part: FOUR classes means at least four candidate rounds, not one.**  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, with a second joining only if it is the same device AND the same semantic class AND covered by the same detector with no new oracle.  A WIDTH guard and a DIRECTION guard sit in the SAME ARM at the same offset and are still different classes -- whether they nonetheless share one fix design is an open question this row does not settle, and it is exactly what the next research pass should answer rather than assume.  HONEST BOUND: reachability here is derived from address-decode reading, NOT measured, except for the WIDTH class -- which now has a rung-3 witness (see `widthkillclass`).  The DIRECTION, BADOFFSET and BADVAL classes have NO witness yet and a round on any of them owes one first.  *** THE COUNT'S SCOPE WAS WRONG AND A SEAT CHECKED IT: 21 IS THE FILE TOTAL, NOT THE HANDLER'S. ***  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` holds **13**.  Per handler: sgi_re 13, sgi_de 1, sgi_mte 1, sgi_de_status 0, helpers 6 -- summing to the 21 this row filed.  So the four-class census spans FOUR HANDLERS, not one, and that matters for scope rather than for tidiness: a round joining sites 'in the same handler' is a different population from one joining sites 'in the same file'.  **This is the dropped-qualifier class again** -- the number was right and the noun was wrong, which is exactly how `b120r`'s NIL and `#464`'s 'floods identically' went wrong.  AND THE WIDTH FIGURE OF 7 IS SHORT BY ONE against the message text: there are EIGHT width-shaped kills in the file, the six in sgi_re plus `sgi_de: len = %i not implemented` and `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]`.  Both are now filed separately -- `sgidewidth` (LIVE, high) and `sgimtewidth` (a mis-service plus a resurrection trap) -- because they sit in different handlers and so cannot join a sgi_re round.  *** READ-ONLY AUDIT, agy seat, 2026-08-30 -- AND THE HEADLINE IS THAT THIS ROUND MAY BE MUCH SMALLER THAN IT LOOKS: ALL SIX ARMS LOOK LIKE **ACCEPT**, NOT DECLINE. ***  The de-fatalisation option set has four members, and the fourth applies where the model already holds the right answer and merely discards it.  **`re_tlb_a`, `re_tlb_b`, `re_tlb_c`, `re_tex`, `re_linear_a` and `re_linear_b` are all WRITTEN by the store path**, so a read can return what was written -- no latch, no message, because there is no event to announce.  *A bounded-announce here would ship a wrong record in product code: printing 'unimplemented; ignored' about a read that is implemented and is not ignored.*  **REACHABILITY: all six are reachable by an ordinary guest load on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`**, and the addresses are derived rather than guessed -- `machine_sgi.c:500` passes base `0x15000000`, `dev_sgi_re_init` registers `sgi_re` at `baseaddr + 0x1000` for `DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH` (0x1000), and the handler restores the full offset with `relative_addr += 0x1000`.  **Every window lies inside `0x15001000-0x150017ff`, none is shadowed, and the `else if` cascade is strictly disjoint.**  Verified here independently by opening `machine_sgi.c` and `crmfbreg.h`: the six constants are `0x1000 / 0x1200 / 0x1400 / 0x1600 / 0x1700 / 0x1780`.  **GUEST KSEG1 ADDRESSES, so a probe author does not re-derive them:** TLB_A `0xb5001000`, TLB_B `0xb5001200`, TLB_C `0xb5001400`, TEX `0xb5001600`, LINEAR_A `0xb5001700`, LINEAR_B `0xb5001780` -- read kills at lines 245, 262, 279, 296, 346, 362 respectively.  **One malloc serves all four handlers** (`dev_sgi_re_init` allocates one `struct sgi_re_data` and hands it to `sgi_de`, `sgi_mte` and `sgi_de_status`), so whatever latch infrastructure `#497` lands is already reachable here.  *The audit and the brief that produced it are preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/audit_20260830/` -- the ledger first cited the `_scratchpad/` copy, which the durable-path rule excludes precisely because it evaporates.  This row now has everything a rung-3 witness needs except the witness itself, which is the point of asking a seat for the addresses rather than the answer.*  *** THIS ROW'S TITLE SAID '21 TERMINATING CALLS INSIDE DEVICE_ACCESS' AND THAT WAS THE SAME CONFLATION I MADE AND CORRECTED ELSEWHERE TODAY.  Corrected 2026-08-30. ***  **21 is the FILE total.  15 are inside a `DEVICE_ACCESS`, and 13 of those in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)`** -- the other 6 live in `draw_primitive` (1) and `do_mte_transfer` (5), neither of which is a `DEVICE_ACCESS` and neither of which a guest reaches by an ordinary access.  *** AND THE TITLE WAS NOT CARELESS -- IT FAITHFULLY REPORTED WHAT THE TOOL SAID, WHICH WAS WRONG. ***  See `exitcensus`: `exit_census.py`'s per-file totals were correct all along, but its BY-ENCLOSING-CONSTRUCT split attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the preceding `DEVICE_ACCESS`.  That bucket is a REACHABILITY claim used for ranking -- *on a guest access path BY CONSTRUCTION* -- so the defect over-stated how many host kills a guest can actually reach.  Fixed; tree-wide the bucket falls **97 -> 83**.  *** THE ACCEPT DESIGN, ADJUDICATED 2026-08-30 -- AND IT REFUTES THE AUDIT RECORDED DIRECTLY ABOVE IT ON ONE ARM OF SIX. ***  **agy ruled ACCEPT for all six read arms on the ground that BACKING STATE EXISTS in the struct.  That is not the ACCEPT test.**  The doctrine's fourth option-set member -- minted from the footbridge IRQEnable precedent -- is *the model already computes the CORRECT answer and merely discards it*.  Existence of state is necessary and nowhere near sufficient.  **FIVE ARMS ARE CLEAN ACCEPT; TEX IS NOT.**  **TLB A/B/C:** the store packs big-endian halfword pairs from raw bus bytes and a readback through the same index expression round-trips BYTE-EXACTLY on this machine -- with a premise that belongs in the comment rather than assumed: `machine_sgi.c` pins BIG ENDIAN, and while the STORE path is byte-order-agnostic the READ path is not.  **LINEAR A/B:** the store splits `idata` into two uint32 cells and the readback is its exact inverse through the `readmax64`/`writemax64` pair -- byte-order symmetric BY CONSTRUCTION, so it needs no endianness premise at all.  *** TEX FAILS THE TEST, AND THE EVIDENCE IS A GREP: `re_tex` HAS ZERO CONSUMERS ANYWHERE. ***  `tlb_a/b/c` feed `horrible_getputpixel`; `linear_a/b` feed it and `do_mte_transfer`; **`re_tex` feeds nothing.**  Its state is written through the `>> 3` index that `sgitexindex` records as a defect -- frames overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells unreachable -- so **the model does not hold the right answer for TEX; it holds a self-consistent WRONG one.**  ACCEPT is available only in the MIRRORED form (the read uses the same `>> 3` expression), which round-trips per frame while shipping hardware-wrong reads at overlapping offsets.  **That is a materially weaker claim than the other five arms', and the round record must not blur them.**  **WHAT ACCEPT OWES THAT DECLINE DOES NOT -- five items, and the second is an ORDERING DEPENDENCY on `#497`:**  (1) **round-trip rows with DISTINCT sentinels per window** -- distinctness catches a cross-wired read, while a same-window index error breaks round-trip because the store frame is position-dependent.  The JOINT translation (store and read shifted together) escapes round-trip BY CONSTRUCTION -- clause (f) territory -- so the contract names it outside, and names honestly that an out-of-decode anchor channel exists for four arms (the rendering and MTE paths consume them) and **does not exist for `re_tex` today**.  (2) **a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control**, because a faithful round-trip through a device is value-indistinguishable from RAM -- the witness ladder's own two-control lesson.  The clean discriminator: **post-`#497` a 4-byte read DECLINES and returns 0 while RAM would serve it.**  So `#497` is an ordering dependency for this round's WITNESS QUALITY, not merely for its latch infrastructure.  (3) **the dead tail's first execution belongs to `#497`, not here.**  A width-declined READ falls through the splice and runs `memory_writemax64` with `odata = 0`.  This round's new surface is narrower than it looks: **NONZERO `odata` through the BE packing**, not 'the tail runs'.  (4) **the claim must be SCOPED**: *returns what the store path recorded*, never *faithful hardware readback*.  TEX refutes the stronger phrase today, and the LINEAR arms store the PROM's own wrongly-sign-extended values verbatim -- returning them faithfully is right; modelling hardware readback is unproven.  **That is `b716a34`'s lesson pre-applied: do not put the strong phrase in a site comment.**  (5) **a PRE-WRITE read row** returning 0, which pins the memset-init as guest-visible ABI and is the minimal de-fatalisation survival row.  *** TEX POISONS THE WARRANT, NOT THE ROUND -- AND THE COUPLING IS ENFORCEABLE BY ONE ROW RATHER THAN BY A DEPENDENCY. ***  Ship TEX MIRRORED plus its round-trip row, and any ONE-SIDED future fix reds that row loudly: store `>> 1` against read `>> 3` writes cells 4-7 and reads 1-4, and the reverse likewise, while the JOINT fix keeps identity and the row stays green.  **So the round-trip row IS the coupling enforcement**, and `sgitexindex`'s fix becomes a declared two-site edit with a tripwire already in place.  Recorded in BOTH rows.  **WHAT IS NOT ACCEPTABLE:** unmirrored `>> 1` reads over the `>> 3` store -- round-trip broken immediately, silent wrong data, worse than a decline -- or ACCEPT recorded with the same warrant strength as the other five arms.  **A LATENT THIS ROUND INHERITS RATHER THAN CREATES:** the TLB arms mask the INITIAL `tlbi` but increment it UNMASKED in the loop, so `relative_addr & 0x1ff >= 0x1fa` with `len 8` runs to index 0x102 -- three halfwords into the adjacent array.  Guest-unreachable for the alignment reason `sgilinearoob` settles path-by-path, and **a mirrored read arm makes it a second SITE of that class** -- record it there so an eventual bound fixes both directions.  *** THE TEST THAT KILLED `sgideoob` WAS THEN APPLIED TO THE SURVIVORS, BY ENUMERATION RATHER THAN BY REASONING -- AND BOTH HOLD.  2026-08-30. ***  Withdrawing a false member is only half the follow-through; the other half is asking whether the same miss is in the rows that remain.  The refuting method for `sgideoob` was *what is this index's ACTUAL RANGE?* -- so every reachable `relative_addr` in each window was walked and the resulting index recorded.  **TLB LOOP** (`re_tlb_a[256]`, `tlbi = ((ra & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff`, four writes at `len 8`): with 8-byte ALIGNMENT -- the only thing a guest can produce -- the maximum index written is **255, IN BOUNDS**.  Unaligned, reachable only by the debugger's `put d`, it is **258, OOB by three halfwords**.  *Exactly as recorded: guest-unreachable, debugger-reachable, same disposition as `sgilinearoob`.*  **`sgitexindex`** (`re_tex[112]`, `tlbi = ((ra & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, window `0x1600-0x16df`): maximum index written is **30, comfortably IN BOUNDS of [112]** -- so this row is NOT an out-of-bounds and never claimed to be.  Its three stated figures were each re-derived independently and all match: **81 cells unreachable**, **3 entries of overlap** between consecutive 8-byte writes.  *** SO THE CLASS AFTER THE WITHDRAWAL IS: TWO REAL OOBs (`sgilinearoob`, the TLB loop), BOTH GUEST-SHIELDED BY ALIGNMENT AND BOTH DEBUGGER-REACHABLE; ONE NON-OOB CORRECTNESS DEFECT (`sgitexindex`); AND ONE WITHDRAWN FALSE MEMBER. ***  Every one now tested by the same method, which is what the false member's whole lesson was.  *** THE WITNESS FIELD WAS STALE AND SAID THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT SHIPPED.  Corrected 2026-08-30 by the pass-2 seat. ***  It read *no rung-3 probe has been built and the round owes one before any edit* -- **false since `#497` shipped six `ld kill` rows**, each a real guest `ld` at one of the six windows, dying with per-site attribution text, on an unmodified machine.  The row's note mentioned them nowhere.  *** AND THE SAME ROWS CHANGE THIS ROW'S COST IN THE OTHER DIRECTION, WHICH MUST BE WRITTEN DOWN NOW RATHER THAN REDISCOVERED: FIXING THIS DEFECT WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING. ***  Six rows' worth.  That is precisely the `n6` shape adjudicated hours earlier in this same session, so this round inherits its ordering obligation: **re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, and only then edit** -- same scripts, opposite colour.  Without that order the re-pin is observationally identical to fitting the test to the binary.  *** THE HEADLINE COUNT INCLUDES ONE SITE THAT CAN NEVER EXECUTE.  Derived 2026-08-30. ***  `dev_sgi_re.c` holds 20 raw `exit(1)` lines; exactly FIVE are commented out (179, 188, 280, 630, 1122) and fifteen are live -- **including 1334, which is inside `sgi_mte`'s unreachable `else` (see `sgimtewidth`).**  **`exit_census.py` excludes COMMENTED-OUT code and cannot see REACHABILITY.**  That is correct behaviour for a text-level tool and it is a limit worth stating rather than discovering: \"live terminating calls\" means SYNTACTICALLY live.  The row's numbers are not wrong -- they are the census's numbers -- but a round quoting them as reachable host kills would be over-claiming by one.  *** THE `sgirekills` / `sgitexindex` COUPLING, ESTABLISHED 2026-08-31 AND RECORDED ON BOTH ROWS SO NEITHER CAN BE CLOSED IN IGNORANCE OF THE OTHER. ***  **The fact, which is settled:** `sgitexindex`'s `>> 1` is correct under BOTH candidate readings of a TEX entry -- 112 halfwords indexed `offset >> 1`, or 28 64-bit entries where entry `k` starts at halfword `4k`, since `(offset >> 3) * 4 == offset >> 1` for 8-aligned offsets.  **So the store's shift is wrong INDEPENDENTLY of the missing datasheet**, and there is no SGI document in `_scratchpad/`.  **Consequence for `sgirekills`:** its TEX read arm can only ACCEPT in the MIRRORED form -- reusing the store's `>> 3` -- because only that round-trips what the store wrote.  Mirroring therefore enshrines a KNOWN-WRONG index in a second place, and gives it a green round-trip row.  *When someone later corrects the shift, that row goes RED because of their correct fix.*  **Consequence for `sgitexindex`, in the other direction:** the TEX read arm is TODAY a live host kill, so **no guest can read back what those writes stored** and the aliasing is currently unobservable from inside the guest.  Fixing `sgirekills` is what would expose it.  That bears on severity and on ORDER, and neither row had recorded it.  *Neither consequence decides the disposition -- that is the question `sgirekills` is held for and a panel is answering it.  What is recorded here is the COUPLING, which is a fact either way.*  *** THE HELD TEX-READ QUESTION IS ANSWERED: DO NOT MIRROR.  3 of 3 packet-fed seats, and the dissenter's FALLBACK is the other two's first choice, so the convergence is total. ***  deepseek and minimax: **decline + latched announce** until the index fix lands.  glm: **BACKED ACCEPT with the CORRECT `>> 1` index**, round-trip oracle pinned to correct semantics and honestly red until `sgitexindex` lands -- *with decline + latched announce as its explicit fallback, and mirrored ACCEPT ranked worst of the three by all three seats.*  **MY PREMISE WAS CIRCULAR AND A SEAT SAID SO USING MY OWN INLINED FINDING.**  See its entry above.  The practical upshot: the choice is between *decline* and *correct-index read*, and MIRRORING -- the option the row was held on -- is off the table.  *One caution against glm's first choice, which it half-concedes: a correct-index read against a wrong-index store means a guest that writes then reads gets data from a different place, which is arguably worse for the guest than a decline returning 0.  That is why all three seats land on decline as at least an acceptable answer.*  **AND ONE OBJECTION OF ITS OWN IS ANSWERED BY A GUARD I FAILED TO INLINE.**  glm marked the *31 of 112 / overlap by three* arithmetic NOT SETTLEABLE because it presupposes 8-byte-wide 8-byte-aligned stores, asserted nowhere in the packet.  Correct as a packet criticism: `#497`'s width guard on this very arm declines `len != 8`, so the width is established, and 8-byte ALIGNMENT follows from the MIPS path (`ld`/`sd` raise ADEL/ADES before `memory_rw`, and the unaligned helper masks the address itself).  **A packet defect of mine, not an analysis defect of its.**",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24937:   "state_note": "*** THE FIX SHIPPED AS `821eb83` ON 2026-08-30, AND THIS FIELD SAID `open` WITH A state_note OPENING 'OPEN, and the committed witness is exactly why it can stay open' UNTIL THE PASS-2 SEAT CAUGHT IT. ***  That is the `es438` shape the carrier quotes verbatim: **a future reader who trusts states reads a shipped, witnessed, measured round as 'not done'.**  Now `in work` with its commit named -- not `closed`, because the round still owes its CHANGELOG block and carries filed coverage debt.  \n\nOPEN, and the committed witness is exactly why it can stay open honestly rather than vaguely.  NO FIX HAS SHIPPED: `sgi_re_width_witness.py` proves the defect and defends nothing.  What remains, in order:  (1) a FIX ROUND on ONE sgi_re width site -- eligible now, since the fix SHAPE is already settled by `#337`'s precedent (latched decline, `(once)` marker), so it is an application of a decided design rather than a fresh design argument;  (2) a rung-3 witness for the pcc2 half, which has NONE -- a read-only seat specified one precisely (`-e mvme187`, CODE 0x10000, r5 = 0xfff42000, one process per fatal row) and said plainly it did not run it;  (3) the class total re-derived if either half moves: 11 today (sgi_re 6 witnessed, pcc2 5 after the M88K alignment narrowing), down from the 16 this row was filed with.  **The witness being committed changes the row's EVIDENCE, not its STATE** -- and the check that caught the ambiguity is right to insist the difference be written down: a row whose detector is in the tree is not unstarted, and a reader seeing `open` with a committed artifact deserves to know which half is done.  *** PASS 1 IS DONE AND THE DESIGN IS SETTLED.  Eight seats answered (agy quota-walled); panel dir `_scratchpad/panel_20260830_180748`.  THE IMPLEMENTER SHOULD READ THIS BEFORE THE BRIEF -- the brief has three known defects the panel repaired. ***  **1. `break` IS NOT LEGAL C AT THESE SITES, and EIGHT OF EIGHT ANSWERING SEATS SAID SO.**  The guards sit in `if`/`else if` arms, not a `switch`, so `break` exits the nearest enclosing loop or switch -- there is none.  The correct edit is an `else if` SPLICE: the decline arm ends and control falls to the common tail.  One structural token changes per site (`if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` becomes `} else if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) {`).  *Unanimity on a defect in the brief is the premise-audit instruction working: the seats were told not to ratify, and none did.*  **2. `return 1`, NOT `return 0` -- and two seats CHECKED it rather than reasoning.**  `memory_rw.c` coerces a 0 return to -1 and the `res <= 0 && !no_exceptions` arm raises `mips_cpu_exception(..., EXCEPTION_DBE, ...)`.  So `return 0` is a guest-visible DATA BUS ERROR, not a decline -- a different design with its own warrant and its own row, and very likely a guest panic on IP32.  **Nobody had checked this in the entire history of the sketch rule that demands it.**  *** 3. THE COMMON TAIL IS DEAD CODE TODAY AND THIS FIX MAKES IT LIVE.  This is a BEHAVIOUR CHANGE the round record must state. ***  `odata` is never assigned anywhere in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` -- it appears exactly twice, at its declaration and at the tail -- and every read path in the function currently `exit(1)`s.  So `memory_writemax64()` at the tail is UNREACHABLE in the shipped tree.  After the fix a declined READ falls through and executes it for the first time, handing the guest a defined 0 instead of an untouched buffer.  That is the right outcome, and **the `else if` form gets it for free while an early `return 1` would not** -- which is precisely the incident the sketch rule was minted from.  **4. THE WITNESS OBLIGATION IS DISCHARGED: 26/26, ALL SIX SITES, BOTH DIRECTIONS**, controls green, sentinel present, artifacts at `regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/opus_p1_20260830/`.  Two seats had split on whether six byte-identical sites are ONE round or SIX; they converged on the operative condition -- extend the witness first -- and then one seat did it.  **Settled by measurement rather than by argument, which is this project's rule for splits.**  With all six witnessed the joining clause is satisfied and ONE round is licensed.  *** 5. TWO L2 ESCAPES ARE ALREADY MEASURED AGAINST THE ROW SET THE BRIEF PROPOSED.  Hand these to the measuring seat as its FIRST assignment, not its ask-again. ***  **M1 -- `len != 8 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE`.**  Every STORE row stays byte-identically green while ALL SIX READ SITES DIE.  The witness probes stores only, so `writeflag` is a dimension no probe value varies -- the same shape as `#496`'s high-byte escape, on a different axis.  Worse, the attribution row reads 0 because the guest then dies at the DIRECTION guard, so even a message-grep row would not attribute the death to this class.  **The missing row is one line: `lw` at each window, post-fix `exit0`** (measured `exit1` on the committed build at all six).  **M2 -- a latch-bit collision.**  Invisible to EVERY one-access-per-session row, because each session forks a fresh emulator with `d->warned` at 0.  Only a TWO-SITES-IN-ONE-SESSION row sees it.  Six single-site rounds would structurally guarantee this blind spot, which is a MEASURABLE argument against splitting.  **M3 (structural, not built) -- the witness's attribution rows use `\"...\" in t`, a SUBSTRING-PRESENCE test.  Deleting the latch entirely passes them.**  That is `#265`'s flood which `#269` had to undo.  Rows must COUNT (`t.count(...) == 1`), not test presence.  **6. PER-SITE LATCHES, with an in-tree warrant rather than a preference.**  `dev_wdc.c`'s own comment adjudicates the discriminator: *SIX FLAGS, NOT FOUR* -- sharing is licensed only across sites MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE AT RUNTIME (`#337`'s shared flag spans two arms of one endianness branch).  The six sgi_re windows are walked in one session by any driver bringing up the RE, so they are not mutually exclusive.  A shared bit reports one window and hides five.  **7. THE FIX'S WARRANT IS STRONGER THAN 'UNIMPLEMENTED', BUT NOT AS STRONG AS THIS POINT ORIGINALLY CLAIMED -- CORRECTED IN PLACE 2026-08-30.**  It said *'the guard is load-bearing HOST memory safety'*.  **MEASURED FALSE by `offsetof`:** the over-run lands INSIDE the same allocation (`re_linear_a[32]` IS `re_linear_b[0]`; `re_linear_b[32]` IS `de_reg[0]`), so it is UB plus SILENT INTRA-STRUCT CROSS-ARRAY CORRUPTION -- not a wild write, not a host crash, and **invisible to plain ASAN**.  The phrase survived here after being corrected in `sgilinearoob`, in the very row whose first line tells the implementer to read it before the brief: *corrected in one place, alive in one more*, which is mechanical rule 2's exact scenario.  **AND THIS POINT BUNDLED A SECOND, SEPARATELY-UNVERIFIED CLAIM:** the `len == 1` over-read of `data[hwi+1]` is of the CALLER'S buffer, and nobody has established that buffer's size -- so it is not 'host memory safety' either.  The honest warrant is **cross-array corruption plus an unverified caller-buffer over-read**, which still settles DECLINE against IMPLEMENT and settles it for reasons that are true.  **8. NAME `sgidewidth` IN THE PRETABLE AND ROUND RECORD.**  It is a SEVENTH live width kill in the same file, deliberately excluded (different handler, different accept set, readable registers).  A round called widthkillclass that fixes 6 of 7 and does not say so writes false unanimity.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31049:     "note": "83df95c PASS 2.  THE ONLY SEAT THAT SETTLED THE CENSUS FROM THE CODE.  It read the data-register arm and derived the pop count as `1 + (len >= 2 ? 1 : 0) + (len == 4 ? 2 : 0)`, confirming widths 1..8 pop 1,2,2,4,2,2,2,2 -- and checked it holds under BOTH endianness branches (identical pop count, only assembly order differs), which nobody asked for.  Then answered the detector question by reading all four touched regress files: no row's oracle depends on the OLD wording, so no detector change is owed.  *** AND IT CORRECTED MY FRAMING AGAINST ITSELF: the old wording 'a 3- or 8-byte access consumes 1 or 2' is VAGUE, NOT FALSE. ***  The true value is 2 and 2 is a member of {1, 2}, so the shipped comment's trailing `\"1 or 2\" until 2026-09-05` reads as a correction of a falsehood when it is really a SHARPENING of an imprecise sentence.  Recorded as a records nuance; the correction improves the comment either way.  No verdict line (format miss).  Panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260906_211839"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31318:   "title": "mipstiny  cpu_mips_coproc.c fpu_subst_tiny(): does classifying tininess on the ENCODED destination word match the MIPS rule, or is after-rounding tininess judged with an UNRESTRICTED exponent range?  THE SOURCE ARGUES ONE WAY AND A REVIEWING SEAT THE OTHER -- unresolved, for the flagship seat",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:32052:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE CODEX SEAT** on the `sgirebufuninit' pass-1 panel, under the standing\n*\"if you found something ELSE, say so\"* licence -- it is not what that brief asked about.\n\n**VERIFIED BY ARITHMETIC BEFORE FILING.**  The two arms disagree about which byte holds which channel:\n\n    PUT  case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:   buf[0] = *color >> 24;  buf[1] = *color >> 16;\n                                  buf[2] = *color >> 8;   buf[3] = 0;\n    GET  case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:   *color = (buf[1] << 24) + (buf[2] << 16) + (buf[3] << 8);\n\nPUT stores the top three bytes at indices **0,1,2**; GET recombines from **1,2,3**.  Storing `0x11223300'\ngives bytes `11 22 33 00', which GET reads back as **`0x22330000'**.  A round trip through the model's own\nRGB mode does not return what was written.\n\n*** THE REASON THIS MATTERS BEYOND THE BUG: EVERY ANALYSIS THIS WEEK, MINE INCLUDED, CALLED THE FOUR\nIMPLEMENTED COLOUR ARMS `CORRECT' AND TREATED ONLY THE `default:' ARMS AS SUSPECT. ***  The\n`sgirebufuninit' brief says so explicitly -- *\"NO colour arm writes buf[4..7] -- not one of the five,\nincluding the four CORRECT ones\"*.  codex's words: *\"Calling all implemented color arms 'correct' is\nunwarranted.\"*  It is right, and the phrase had been repeated unchecked across three documents.\n\nWHICH SIDE IS WRONG IS **NOT** ESTABLISHED HERE and must not be guessed: RGBA uses 0,1,2,3 symmetrically on\nboth sides, and ABGR uses 0..3 reversed on both sides, so RGB is the only asymmetric pair -- but whether the\nhardware packs RGB at the low three bytes or the high three is a DATASHEET question this row does not\nanswer.  A round taking it owes that reference, or a comparison against a real framebuffer dump.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability is not in doubt (RGB is documented and implemented) but nothing here shows a guest\ndriver round-tripping through it.\n\n*** WHICH SIDE IS WRONG IS NOW SETTLED, AND NO DATASHEET WAS NEEDED.  THE ANSWER WAS ONE FILE AWAY, IN THE\nCONSUMER OF THESE VERY BYTES.  Derived 2026-09-07, same day as the filing. ***\n\nThis row said the packing question needed *\"a datasheet reference or a comparison against a real framebuffer\ndump\"*.  **It needed neither.**  `dev_sgi_gbe.c`'s tick reads the SAME tile memory these arms write and turns\nit into host framebuffer pixels:\n\n    color = (buf[i]<<24) + (buf[i+1]<<16) + (buf[i+2]<<8) + buf[i+3];\n\nSo the in-tree contract is unambiguous: **byte index 0 is the MOST significant channel.**  Against that:\n\n    PUT  RGB    buf[0]=c>>24  buf[1]=c>>16  buf[2]=c>>8   buf[3]=0        AGREES with the consumer\n    PUT  RGBA   buf[0]=c>>24  buf[1]=c>>16  buf[2]=c>>8   buf[3]=c        AGREES\n    GET  RGBA   (buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8)+buf[3]              AGREES\n    GET  ABGR   (buf[3]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[1]<<8)+buf[0]              AGREES (deliberately reversed)\n    GET  RGB    (buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8)                     *** DISAGREES -- off by one ***\n\n**THE DEFECT IS IN THE `GET' ARM, AND IT IS THE ONLY ARM IN EITHER SWITCH THAT DISAGREES WITH THE CONSUMER.**\nIts sibling `GET RGBA' does the identical job one index lower and is correct, which is about as strong an\ninternal warrant as this tree offers.  The fix is the index triple: `(buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8)'.\n\n**WHY THIS MATTERS FOR THE ROUND'S SHAPE:** it converts an open design question into a one-line fix with a\nnamed oracle, and it supplies the detector's channel for free -- a PUT-then-GET round trip through RGB must\nreturn what was written, which is a value oracle, not a survival one.\n\n*The general lesson, and it is the option-set rule wearing another hat: I reached for an EXTERNAL authority\nbefore asking whether the tree already answered the question.  `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf' confirms there is no SGI\nor CRIME document here at all -- only MIPS manuals -- so the external route was not merely slower, it was\nCLOSED, and the in-tree consumer was the only available oracle the whole time.*\n\n*** A STRONGER ARGUMENT THAN THE CONSUMER ONE, AND IT NEEDS NO EXTERNAL REFERENCE AT ALL.  Enumerated\n2026-09-07 by walking all four modes rather than reasoning about them. ***\n\nThe note above identifies the GET arm as wrong using `dev_sgi_gbe.c` as an oracle.  That argument stands, but\nit depends on how GBE INTERPRETS an assembled value -- so here is the same conclusion from inside the\nfunction, depending on nothing outside it:\n\n    mode   PUT writes            GET returns    round-trips?\n    RGB    11 22 33 00           0x22330000     *** NO -- expected 0x11223300 ***\n    RGBA   11 22 33 44           0x11223344     yes\n    ABGR   44 33 22 11           0x11223344     yes\n\n**RGB IS THE ONLY MULTI-BYTE MODE THAT FAILS ITS OWN ROUND TRIP.**  And the two that pass do so using\nOPPOSITE byte orders -- RGBA ascending, ABGR descending -- which proves the file can express either\nconvention correctly.  So this is not a disagreement about endianness or about what the hardware packs; **RGB\nis simply inconsistent with itself**, and no datasheet is needed to say so.\n\n**AND IT NARROWS WHICH SIDE:** `PUT RGB' writes the top three bytes to `buf[0..2]', exactly as `PUT RGBA'\ndoes; `GET RGBA' reads `buf[0..3]' and is correct.  Since the two PUTs agree on `buf[0..2]', a correct\n`GET RGB' must read `buf[0..2]' too.  **It reads `buf[1..3]'.**  The GET arm is the outlier by the file's own\ninternal evidence.\n\n*** ONE THING THE ENUMERATION CORRECTED, AND IT WOULD HAVE READ AS A SECOND DEFECT: `CI' ALSO FAILS A NAIVE\nROUND TRIP, AND THAT IS CORRECT BEHAVIOUR. ***  `CI' is COLOUR-INDEX -- one byte -- so `PUT' storing only\n`buf[0]' and `GET' returning only `buf[0]' loses the upper 24 bits BY DESIGN.  A round-trip oracle applied\nblindly to all four modes would red on CI and manufacture a phantom defect.  **The detector must expect CI to\npreserve ONE BYTE, not four** -- which is exactly the kind of row that looks like a bug and is a\nmisunderstanding of the mode.\n\n**THE DETECTOR FALLS OUT OF THIS FOR FREE:** one PUT-then-GET row per mode, with RGBA and ABGR as ACCEPT rows\nthat must stay green (they are the de-escalation clause's accept side, already present) and CI pinned at one\nbyte.  The value oracle is the round trip itself -- no survival row, no exit-status channel, and nothing that\na message change could silence.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN, AND IT CAN MEASURE WHICH ARM IS WRONG RATHER THAN INFERRING IT.  Derived\n2026-09-07 from the XFER path. ***\n\nEverything above identifies the GET arm by ARGUMENT -- the in-tree consumer, and the fact that RGB alone\nfails its own round trip.  Both are sound.  **But the model makes the question directly measurable, and a\nmeasurement beats two good arguments.**\n\n`draw_primitive()` reads TWO INDEPENDENT guest registers:\n\n    dst_mode = d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_MODE_DST - 0x2000) / 4];\n    src_mode = d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC - 0x2000) / 4];\n\nand an XFER op **GETs with `src_mode` and PUTs with `dst_mode`** -- so the colour type of the read and of the\nwrite are separately selectable by the guest.  That yields a 2x2 that ISOLATES the defective arm:\n\n    src_mode   dst_mode   what it tests                       expected if GET RGB is the wrong one\n    RGBA       RGBA       CONTROL -- both arms known good     destination == source\n    RGB        RGBA       isolates the RGB **GET**            destination CORRUPTED\n    RGBA       RGB        isolates the RGB **PUT**            destination == source (PUT is fine)\n    RGB        RGB        the full defect, both arms          destination corrupted\n\n**The predicted corruption is deterministic and computable in advance**, which is what makes this a value\noracle rather than an inequality: `GET RGB` builds `(buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8)` from source bytes\n`B0 B1 B2 B3`, giving `B1 B2 B3 00`.  So a source of `0xaabbccdd` copied through RGB lands as **`0xbbccdd00`**\n-- a one-byte left shift with a zero fill.  A row can assert that exact value, not merely that something\nchanged.\n\n**AND THE 2x2 IS THE DE-ESCALATION CLAUSE'S ACCEPT SIDE FOR FREE**: the RGBA/RGBA row and the RGBA/RGB row\nmust stay GREEN after the fix, so the accept rows the clause demands are the same rows that isolate the\ndefect.  No extra sessions.\n\n**THE MODE WORDS**, from the same arithmetic as [[sgirebufuninit]]: LIN_A is bits 10-12 = `0x1000`, the depth\nfield is bits 8-9, the colour type bits 4-7.  With `BUFDEPTH_32` (field 2, `0x200`) so all four bytes\ntransfer:\n\n    RGBA   0x1220     RGB   0x1210\n\n`0x1220` is exactly what `regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py` already programs, and its `copy lin_a` row\nalready performs an XFER copy and reads the destination back.  **So the witness is that probe's existing\nsession with one register changed** -- which is why this is the cheapest live item on the board.\n\n*** ONE THING TO CHECK BEFORE TRUSTING THE 2x2, and it is the trap that caught the last round: does the XFER\npath actually reach `horrible_getputpixel` twice, once per mode?  ***  The GET is guarded by\n`drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN`, and there is a SECOND `horrible_getputpixel(0, ...)` immediately above it\nthat reads the DESTINATION with `dst_mode` for the ROP path.  If that one also fires, three calls occur and\nthe 2x2's attribution is wrong.  **Enumerate the drawmode bits the session sets before building the rows** --\nthe previous round's witness failed twice on exactly this kind of unexamined session detail.\n\n*** THE WITNESS OBJECTIONS THAT KILLED [[sgirebufuninit]]'s DESIGN WERE CHECKED AGAINST THIS ROW AND DO\nNOT TRANSFER.  Checked 2026-09-07, not assumed. ***\n\nThe codex seat rejected `sgirebufuninit''s witness on two grounds.  Both were tested here:\n\n* **\"Changing that shared constant changes the observation path and the control simultaneously.\"**  True\n  there, because `MODE_LIN_A' serves the destination, the matched control AND a source case.  **Not true\n  here:** `setup(A, src_mode)' stores `DE_MODE_DST = MODE_LIN_A' (hardcoded) and `DE_MODE_SRC = src_mode'\n  (the parameter).  Passing `0x1210' changes ONLY the source mode; the destination and the control keep\n  `MODE_LIN_A' untouched.\n* **\"Separate automatic objects have no architectural promise of retaining each other's contents\"**, and\n  \"observing an unassigned byte does not guarantee that its value differs from the intended padding\".\n  Decisive against an uninitialised-memory oracle.  **This row's oracle reads no unassigned byte at all:**\n  a `PUT RGB' followed by a `GET RGB' on a KNOWN source is fully defined behaviour, and\n  `0xaabbccdd -> 0xbbccdd00' is a deterministic byte re-index, not a stale-value comparison.\n\n**So this row keeps a real value oracle while `sgirebufuninit' needs initializedness instrumentation** --\nwhich is why the two rounds are different in cost by a wide margin, and why this one is next despite the\nother's higher profile earlier tonight.\n\n*Recorded here so the round that takes this does not have to re-derive whether a rejection aimed at a\nneighbouring row applies to it.  A refutation is as transferable as a warrant, and needs checking the same\nway.*\n\n*** PASS 1 PANEL, 2026-09-07 (_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055).  THE PANEL REFUTED MY ATTRIBUTION, A DIFFERENT FILE THEN\nSETTLED IT, AND A SEAT CAUGHT A DEFECT IN MY OWN ORACLE. ***\n\n**1. THE ATTRIBUTION WAS NOT ESTABLISHED, and four seats said so independently.**  My brief argued the\nGET is the wrong arm on two grounds:\n  * **Ground 1 was CIRCULAR** -- *\"PUT's `buf[3] = 0` is an explicit pad write, so a GET reading byte 3\n    reads the pad\"* assumes PUT is authoritative, which is precisely the question.\n  * **Ground 2 was neutralised** -- I appealed to RGBA's byte order, but ABGR uses the opposite one and\n    the default/raw GET arm uses ABGR order too, so the file offers no single order to appeal to.\n  * **The measurement is ARM-NEUTRAL** -- 0xbbccdd00 is what the code does; under a pad-FIRST container\n    it is the FAITHFUL result and PUT is the bug.  A round-trip failure cannot say which end failed.\n\n**2. THE SETTLING FACT IS IN `dev_sgi_gbe.c`, AND NO SEAT FOUND IT.**  The scanout reads the same tile\nmemory back for display:\n\n    color = (buf[i]<<24) + (buf[i+1]<<16) + (buf[i+2]<<8) + buf[i+3];   /* byte 0 = MSB */\n    case CRMFB_MODE_TYP_RGB8:      // Used by NetBSD's X11 server\n            *r = color >> 24;  *g = color >> 16;  *b = color >> 8;      /* byte 3 UNUSED */\n\n**R = byte 0, G = byte 1, B = byte 2, byte 3 ignored -- exactly what the PUT arm writes.**  The pad sits\nat buf[3] because the CONSUMER ignores buf[3], not because PUT writes a zero there.  Different file,\nopposite direction, independent of which DE arm is right: that is what makes it non-circular, and it is\nthe answer to the Q2 I asked and nobody could reach from the packet.\n\n**3. glm INDEPENDENTLY DISSOLVED THE ABGR OBJECTION**, which is the sharpest single contribution of the\npanel: *\"for the same color, RGBA and ABGR write IDENTICAL memory bytes, [R,G,B,A]; they differ only in\nregister word order ... among the five 4-byte arms, the RGB GET is the lone container outlier -- your\ncase is stronger than you stated.\"*  Verified by reading: RGBA word 0xRRGGBBAA -> buf [RR,GG,BB,AA];\nABGR word 0xAABBGGRR -> buf [RR,GG,BB,AA].  **My \"opposite byte orders\" was about the register WORD.  The\nquestion is about the memory CONTAINER, and on the container all three 4-byte PUT arms agree.**\n\n**4. *** THE PANEL CAUGHT MY ORACLE, AND IT WOULD HAVE FAILED A CORRECT FIX. *** **  The witness's\nsuccess row pinned **0xaabbccdd**, the source word.  A corrected RGB GET returns **0xaabbcc00** -- RGB\ncarries three channels and the pad is not transported.  **Four seats named 0xaabbcc00 independently**\n(grok x5, glm x3, kimi-substitute x2, codex x2) against a brief that had said otherwise.  Re-pinned\nBEFORE the code changed, and the committed build re-measured RED under the new expectation -- the\noracle-correction rule's order discriminator, satisfied rather than asserted.\n\n*The row that survives is stronger for it: `rgb chan` pins the EXACT post-fix value, so a fix that moves\nthe bytes to some THIRD wrong place still reds -- which a \"not the corrupt value\" row could not do.*\n\n**5. codex's RESERVATION IS KEPT AND IS IN THE CONTRACT:** *\"GET is the best-supported repair site for\nthis emulator's RGB layout; hardware fidelity and real-driver usage remain unproved.  The cited MTE RGBA\nbranch is unreachable.\"*  No CRIME/RE datasheet is in `_scratchpad/` (checked by `ls`), so this ships as\nan INTERNAL-CONSISTENCY fix warranted by the tree's own consumer -- **not a claim about silicon**.\n\n**6. glm's MTE `|=` HAZARD -- checked, and its antecedent is FALSE.**  glm warned that\n`src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA` yields ABGR (0x30) *if the type field already holds RGB (0x10)*, and said\nplainly it could not settle it from the packet, naming the settling fact.  I checked it:\n`src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & 0x300) >> 8) << 8)` sets bits >=10 and bits 8-9 only -- **the type\nnibble is never set**, so `|= 0x20` gives exactly RGBA.  The hazard does not fire.  *It is still a real\nlatent note -- if that construction ever gained a type field, `|=` would corrupt it rather than replace\nit -- and codex separately confirmed the whole branch is unreachable anyway (`sgimtedepth4dead`).*\n\n**SEATS: 6 of 7 scriptable answered.**  codex 233 KB, grok 12 KB, glm 9.9 KB, minimax 6.8 KB,\nkimi-substitute 6.9 KB, deepseek 2.7 KB.  The **kimi CLI is 403-walled** on its weekly window (350-byte\nfile, correctly reported WALLED rather than scored as a short answer) and its seat is **COVERED** by the\n`kimi-k3:cloud` substitute.  **agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED** -- measurement reserve, owner ruling.\n**Opus 5 and Fable 5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED** -- both held entire for the 2026-09-09 adjudication by\nstanding owner directive.  *Three seats unfired for three different stated reasons; none is a seat\nfailure and none is agreement.*\n\n*** KILL TABLE 1 FOUND A CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH IN MY OWN DETECTOR.  L2 -- REPAIRED IN-ROUND. ***\n(`regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/killtable_20260907/`, 6-row detector)\n\n    base            6/6\n    M1_verbatim     4/6   KILLED  -- clause (b), by BOTH rgb rows\n    M2_off1         5/6   KILLED\n    M3_pad          5/6   KILLED -- by rgb chan ONLY\n    M4_or           6/6   EQUIVALENT, proven -- not a coverage gap\n    M5_rev          5/6   KILLED\n    M6_cond         6/6   *** CLEAN SWEEP -- FLOOR BREACH ***\n    nullmedium      1/6   clause (c) discharged\n\n**M6_cond is the canonical clause-(d) form** -- guard KEPT, repair CONDITIONED on a field the guarded\nbody reads:\n\n    case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:\n            if (bufdepth == 4)  *color = (buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8);   /* the fix   */\n            else                *color = (buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8);   /* the defect */\n\n**Every session in the 6-row detector used `BUFDEPTH_32`, so `bufdepth` was 4 in all of them and the\nmutant's FIX branch is the only one the detector ever executed.**  The detector proved the fix works\nexactly where it was measured and said nothing whatever about anywhere else.\n\n`bufdepth` is squarely inside the DEPENDS-ON closure and not an exotic choice: it is read by the guarded\nbody (it is `memory_rw`'s transfer length) and it comes from **bits 8-9 of the SAME mode word** whose\nbits 4-7 the guard reads.  The closure enumerates it first, not last.\n\n**REPAIR: `rgb depth64`, a second session at `BUFDEPTH_64` (bufdepth 8).**  Width 8 keeps `buf[0..2]`\nfilled by `memory_rw`, so the row stays a VALUE oracle; widths 1 and 2 would leave `buf[1..2]`\nuninitialised, which is `sgirebufuninit` and would make the row non-deterministic.  **8 is the only\nother width that can carry a value oracle at all**, which is why the repair is one row rather than a\nsweep of the axis.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR OWES ITS OWN MIRROR, WHICH IS WHY TABLE 2 CARRIES `M7_cond8`. ***  The class-closure\namendment says a repair made at exactly the address the mutant used leaves the symmetric cell open --\n`#499` paid for that three times running.  M6 exempts the width the OLD rows use; M7 exempts the width\nthe NEW row uses.  If M7 escapes, the repair reproduced the defect it was built for, and the honest\ndisposition is to close the WIDTH AXIS in one diff rather than add a third cell.\n\n**Table 2 also carries `M8_put`, which measures rather than asserts what `rgb samemode` bought.**  Two\nseats said a same-mode row would reach the RGB PUT arm that nothing else executes; M8 corrupts that arm\nand nothing else.  *A row added on a seat's say-so and never measured is an assumption wearing a row's\nclothes.*\n\n*** PASS 2: THE glm SEAT BURNED, AND ITS ANSWER IS THE BEST CRITIQUE OF THE WARRANT ANYONE HAS\nGIVEN.  Recovered to `regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/glm_BURN_thinking_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**THE BURN, by the carrier's own discriminator:** `\"response\": \"\"`, `thinking` 114,797 chars,\n`done_reason: \"stop\"` -- the model FINISHED, so the budget did not run out; the answer simply never\nreached the response channel.  **A size check scores this the best answer in the panel (120 kB, second\nonly to codex) and a naive check scores it DOWN.**  The carrier's rule is what got it read: an empty\n`response` is the diagnostic, `done_reason` decides the disposition, and **the thinking is read even\nwhen there is no channel marker.**\n\n**IT ANSWERED MY HEADLINE Q1 AND THE ANSWER IS THAT MY WORRY WAS UNFOUNDED:** *\"The\nguest-programming concern is a red herring -- the warrant compares two STATIC interpretations\nhard-coded in the emulator (GBE's RGB8 byte->bit mapping; DE PUT's register->byte mapping).  Guest\nprogramming selects which interpretation each device applies but cannot change either mapping, so no\nguest-consistency assumption is needed for the layouts to agree.\"*\n\n**AND THEN IT FOUND WHAT I HAD SMUGGLED IN -- three facts the packet asserts and never shows:**\n  1. **that GBE scans the SAME memory the DE writes** -- *\"asserted twice, never inlined.  If GBE\n     scans a copy or a different aperture, the warrant collapses to 'two devices with\n     similar-looking code'\"*;\n  2. **that the RGB PUT writes R at `buf[0]`** -- only the comment asserts it, and the diff does not\n     include the PUT arm;\n  3. the GBE RGB8 bit-position claim itself.\n  4. *(noted, unfalsifiable)* \"authoritative consumer\" presumes the GBE code is correct, and two\n     agreeing paths by the same author share blind spots.\n\n*** THE DECOMPOSITION IS THE VALUABLE PART AND IT MAKES THE ROUND'S POSITION STRONGER, NOT WEAKER: ***\n*\"the fix is round-trip-correct for ANY channel order ... So the fix is SAFE ON ROUND-TRIP GROUNDS\nALONE; the GBE warrant is only needed for the ARM-CHOICE, and it stands or falls with fact (2).\"*\n\n**That separates two claims I had run together.**  The FIX's correctness follows from PUT/GET\nsymmetry and needs no external warrant at all.  The GBE evidence is needed only to justify editing\nthe GET rather than the PUT.  A future reader who doubts the scanout argument still has a correct\nfix; they only lose the reason for the arm choice.\n\n**FACT (1) CHECKED AND CONFIRMED, with a qualification glm could not reach from a packet.**  Both\ndevices address the same space by the same formula:\n\n    GBE   tile[i] = (256*ptr[i*2] + ptr[i*2+1]) << 16;\n          memory_rw(..., base + 512*line, buf, len,      MEM_READ,  NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL);\n    DE    tileptr = d->re_tlb_a[tile_nr] << 16;\n          memory_rw(..., tileptr + ofs,   buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL);\n\nSame `<< 16` on a 16-bit tile pointer, same PHYSICAL path.  **The qualification: WHICH tiles each\ntouches comes from separately-programmed pointers** (GBE's tiletable in memory, DE's `re_tlb_a`\narray), so the honest statement is *\"whatever tile the guest points both at, DE writes and GBE reads\nwith the same byte-to-address mapping\"* -- which is what a framebuffer driver does and is enough for\nthe arm choice.\n\n**FACT (2) IS A NAMING CLAIM, AND THE WARRANT DOES NOT NEED IT.**  glm is right that only the comment\ncalls `buf[0]` \"R\".  The tighter form drops the channel names entirely: **byte 0 carries bits 31..24\nin BOTH directions** -- DE PUT writes `*color >> 24` there, GBE's RGB8 reads bits 31..24 out of the\nword it composes with `buf[0]` as MSB.  The chain closes on BIT POSITIONS; the channel names are\ndecoration.  **The shipped comment is accurate in the tree's own vocabulary but over-specified**, and\nthe bit-position form is what a future reader should get -- an L1 tightening, since the round has not\nshipped.\n\n*This is the third time this session that the most useful finding came from a seat that a size or\nstatus check would have mis-scored: the kimi CLI walled and its substitute answered, grok looked\nstarved at 354 bytes and was mid-answer, and glm looked like the panel's best answer and had an empty\nresponse field.*\n\n*** PASS 2 FOUND THREE DEFECTS IN MY OWN RECORDS AND ONE MIS-BUILT MUTANT.  The kimi SUBSTITUTE\n-- a PACKET-FED transport that cannot read the repo -- found all four from the packet's own\narithmetic. ***  Panel `_scratchpad/panel_20260907_104814`.\n\n**FINDING 1 -- MY \"MIRROR\" MUTANT WAS NOT A MIRROR, and this is the serious one.**\n\n    kimi: \"M7_cond8 cannot be both 'the mirror of M6' and score 7/8; a mirror predicts 5/8.\"\n\n    M6_cond   if (bufdepth == 4) FIXED else DEFECT   ->  width 4 fixed, width 8 defective\n    M7_cond8  if (bufdepth != 8) FIXED else DEFECT   ->  width 4 fixed, width 8 defective\n\n**Over the only two widths any row drives, {4, 8}, those are the SAME FUNCTION.**  M7 re-measured\nM6 and I recorded it as having closed the symmetric cell.  It had measured nothing new.\n\n*** MEASURED, table 3 (`killtable3_20260907/`): the TRUE mirror `M9_truemirror`\n(`if (bufdepth == 8) FIXED else DEFECT`) scores **5/8**, EXACTLY the score kimi derived from the\npacket. ***  Three width-4 rows red.  **So the width axis IS closed -- but by `M9`, not by the\nmutant the earlier record credited.**  The conclusion survives; the evidence for it did not, and\na claim resting on a mutant that measures nothing is a wrong record however right its conclusion.\n\n**FINDING 2 -- THE LOCATION AXIS, and the measurement went the other way from the prediction.**\nkimi named `ofs` as an unpinned clause-(d) axis and predicted `if (ofs == K)` sweeps 8/8, reasoning\nthat every row writes at ofs 0.  **`M10_ofs` (`if (ofs == 0) FIXED else DEFECT`) reds three rows\ninstead.**  The reason is a fact about the probe that the packet did not contain: the source pixel\nis read at `src_x = SRC_OFS = 0x40`, so `ofs` is 0x40 on every GET and 0 only on the destination\nPUT.  **The rows discriminate on location already -- by an accident of the probe's geometry rather\nthan by design**, which is worth recording as exactly that: covered, not intended, and therefore\nfragile to anyone who moves SRC_OFS.\n\n**FINDING 3 -- THE \"SAME SCRIPT\" CLAIM IS CONTRADICTED BY ITS OWN DENOMINATORS.**\n\n    kimi: \"S4's 'same script' is contradicted by its own denominators (4/6 -> 8/8).\"\n\nCorrect.  The witness ladder's clause (i) wants ONE script red pre-fix and green post-fix; I quoted\na 6-row pre-fix run against an 8-row post-fix run.  Table 3 measures `prefix8row` -- the shipped\n8-row probe against pre-fix source -- so the pair is genuinely same-script.\n\n**FINDING 4 (L4, CORRECTED IN BOTH PLACES) -- THE GATE COMMENT'S \"SILENTLY GREEN\" IS FALSE.**\nThe sibling block says a row name of 16+ chars makes the two-space pattern *\"UNSATISFIABLE, which is\nthe same trap in its other, SILENT direction: the check stops testing anything and stays green.\"*\nI copied that verbatim into the new block.  **kimi refuted it and the measurement agrees:** `count`\nreturns 0, `check` compares 0 against a wanted \"1\", and 0 != 1 FAILS -- loudly.  Rendered rows at\nlengths 15/16/17 match 1/0/0.  The unsatisfiable direction is SAFE for a want-1 check and silent\nonly where a check wants ZERO, which no row check here does.  Corrected in MY block (L1) and in the\nSIBLING's (L4, wrong record, which the stopping rule licenses).\n\n**minimax's axis, recorded and measured:** *\"the detector does NOT catch a PUT/GET pair that swap\nchannel bytes consistently, and does NOT exercise ABGR.\"*  The first is TRUE AND UNAVOIDABLE -- a\nconsistent swap round-trips by construction, so no round-trip oracle can see it, and that is a\nclause-(e) disclosure rather than a gap.  `M11_cmode` measures the colour-type conditioning form.\n\n*** AND THE SEAT THAT FOUND ALL FOUR CANNOT READ THE REPO. ***  Every one came from the packet's own\narithmetic -- comparing two mutant predicates, comparing two denominators, and re-deriving what an\nunsatisfiable grep does to a `check`.  **The doctrine says a packet-fed seat supplies attack\nreasoning and never repo fact, and that is exactly what happened**: it made no claim about the tree\nand four claims about my arithmetic, and was right about all four.\n\n*** THE REMAINING PASS-2 SEATS, READ IN FULL RATHER THAN BY VERDICT LINE -- WHICH IS HOW THE ONE\nNEW AXIS WAS FOUND. ***\n\n**AN EXTRACTOR BLIND SPOT, worth fixing separately:** `seat_verdicts.py` filed deepseek as *\"answered,\nNO VERDICT LINE (format miss)\"*.  **It has one** -- it just reads `VERDICT:` rather than `**VERDICT:`,\nso the pattern missed it.  Had I trusted the extractor's label, a seat with four substantive claims\nwould have been filed as a formatting problem.  *That is the unread-review class arriving through a\nTOOL rather than through inattention.*\n\n**deepseek made TWO claims that measurement refutes and ONE that is new and good.**\n\n  * **REFUTED -- *\"the gate block's two-space grep is unsatisfiable for every name shorter than\n    15\"*.**  It has the direction backwards.  A name of N chars is followed by `(16-N)+1` spaces, so\n    every N <= 15 leaves at least two and matches.  Measured over the shipped row names: live(4),\n    devsig(6), copy lin_a(10), rgb chan(8), rgb samemode(12), rgb not corrupt(15) -- **all six\n    match**.  Unsatisfiability begins at N >= 16, which is the case the block warns about.\n  * **REFUTED -- *\"`rgb not corrupt` is vacuous as inlined\"*.**  Table 2 measures it firing: it is one\n    of the FOUR rows that redden `M1_verbatim` (4/8).  It declines to fire on `M3_pad`, but that is\n    SPECIFICITY, not vacuity -- the row names one exact wrong value and M3 produces a different one,\n    which is why `rgb chan` exists beside it.\n  * *** NEW AND WORTH MEASURING -- the `tileptr` axis. ***  Its Q2: *\"Condition the corrected RGB GET\n    on `ofs == 0` (or on `tileptr == <the single value the probe uses>`).\"*  The `ofs` half was\n    measured and DIES (`M10_ofs` 4/8, because the source is read at `src_x = 0x40`).  **The `tileptr`\n    half is untested and is not the same question**: every row in this detector drives LIN_A, so a\n    repair conditioned on the ADDRESSING MODE -- e.g. `tileptr & 0x80000000`, the linear flag -- is\n    true for all eight rows and would sweep clean.  `tileptr` is read by the guarded body, so it is\n    inside the DEPENDS-ON closure, and \"condition the fix on linear-vs-tiled\" is a plausible edit\n    rather than a contrived one.  **Queued for a fourth table.**\n\n**grok's verdict, and it is the sharpest framing of the round:** *\"RATIFY THE ONE-LINE GET FIX; DO NOT\nRATIFY S2 AS STATED, THE DETECTOR'S CONTRACT TEXT, OR 'WIDTH IS THE ONLY OPEN (d) AXIS.'\"*  All three\nreservations are upheld by measurement: S2 needed the qualification four seats independently named;\nthe contract text overclaims; and width was NOT the only open axis -- location and addressing mode\nwere both unexamined, one of which is now measured closed and one of which is queued.\n\n*** FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY NAMED THE SAME GAP IN S2 -- glm, kimi, minimax and deepseek -- and that\nunanimity IS worth something here, unlike unanimity on a brief-supplied fact: each derived it from the\npacket's own internal inconsistency (the DE mode register and the GBE mode register are different\nregisters) rather than from agreeing with me. ***\n\n**KILL TABLE 3 (`killtable3_20260907/`) -- every mutant pass 2 demanded, all killed, and the\nclause-(i) gap closed:**\n\n    base            8/8\n    M9_truemirror   5/8   the ACTUAL mirror -- exactly the score kimi derived from the packet\n    M10_ofs         4/8   location axis, killed (see below -- not for the reason predicted)\n    M11_cmode       7/8   colour-type conditioning, killed\n    prefix8row      4/8   *** the shipped 8-row probe against PRE-FIX source ***\n\n**`prefix8row` is the same-script pair the ladder wants** -- 4/8 red before, 8/8 green after, one\nprobe.  The earlier `4/6 -> 8/8` was two probes, which pass 2 caught from the denominators alone.\n\nRestore verified against SEC; the clone sentinel cleared.\n\n*** KILL TABLE 4 (`killtable4_20260907/`): A SECOND CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH. ***\n\n    base          8/8\n    M12_linear    8/8   *** CLEAN SWEEP -- the addressing-mode axis was wholly unpinned ***\n\nEvery row drove LIN_A, so `linear` (`tlb_mode > 3`) was true in all eight and a repair conditioned on\nit was invisible.  `linear` DOMINATES the edited lines via `if (!linear && ...) return;`, so it is in\nthe DEPENDS-ON closure -- **L2, repaired in-round** by `rgb tiled`, a ninth row driving a TLB_A\nsource (src tiled, dst LIN_A deliberately: the arm under test is the GET, whose `linear` comes from\n`src_mode`).\n\n**The seat's FORM had to be corrected to make it measurable.**  deepseek named `tileptr`; that cannot\ndiscriminate, because `tileptr &= ~0x80000000` strips the flag before the switch.  *A prediction is\nnot a measurement until someone finds the variable the source actually keeps.*\n\n*** AND THE NEW ROW REPORTED MY OWN SETUP ERROR RATHER THAN PASSING QUIETLY. ***  First run: it read\n`0x77665544` = `FG_VAL`, which the probe's own `DE_FG` comment names as what a DECLINED get leaves.\n`re_tlb_a` is `uint16_t[256]` written by a halfword loop, not `uint32_t` like `re_linear_a`, so the\n`<< 32` shift correct for LINEAR_A lands in `re_tlb_a[2]` and leaves `[0]` zero -- `tileptr = 0`,\naccess declined.  Corrected to `<< 48`.  **A green row there would have been vacuous by\nconstruction: it would have measured a declined access rather than a tiled one.**\n\n**`rgb tiled` WENT RED TWICE BEFORE IT WENT GREEN, and both times it read `0x77665544` = FG_VAL,\nwhich this probe's own DE_FG comment names as what a DECLINED get leaves.**\n\n  1. **`re_tlb_a` is `uint16_t[256]` filled by a HALFWORD loop**, not `uint32_t` like `re_linear_a`.\n     An 8-byte store fills `[0..3]` from four big-endian halfwords, so `[0]` comes from bits 63..48.\n     The `<< 32` shift that is CORRECT for RE_LINEAR_A lands in `re_tlb_a[2]`.\n  2. *** A TILED TILE POINTER MUST CARRY ITS OWN VALID BIT. ***  `if (!(tileptr & 0x80000000))\n     return;` gates every access.  LIN_A sets the bit EXPLICITLY (`0x80000000 | (re_linear_a[n] <<\n     12)`), so a probe built from the linear path never learns it exists; TLB_A takes it from the\n     stored value, and since `re_tlb_a` is uint16_t, bit 31 of tileptr IS bit 15 of the register.\n     Fixed by `re_tlb_a[0] = 0x8002` -> tileptr 0x80020000 -> valid -> masked to 0x20000.\n\n**A row that had gone green through either mistake would have been VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION** --\nmeasuring a declined access while claiming to measure a tiled one.  It went red instead, twice, and\nnamed the value that explained why.\n\n*And a lesson about the breach: `M12_linear` swept 8/8 precisely because no row had ever driven the\nnon-linear path -- and closing it required two device facts the linear path conceals.  **An axis\nnothing exercises is also an axis nobody has had to learn**, which is why the cost of closing one is\nsystematically underestimated.*\n\n*** PASS 3 (panel_20260907_121317) REFUSED TO CLOSE THE CHAIN.  FIVE SEATS, ONE REASON, AND A\nTHIRD FLOOR BREACH. ***\n\n    codex   NOT ESTABLISHED YET | OPEN (split)\n    grok    NOT RATIFY -- do not close this chain\n    kimi-sub NOT CLOSED\n    deepseek NOT CLOSED\n    minimax  rgb tiled is NOT proven sound; the floor is NOT closed\n    glm     *** SEAT DOWN -- SECOND THINKING BURN OF THE DAY *** (121,216 chars recovered by hand to\n            `glm_BURN2_thinking_20260907.txt`; it was reconstructing the per-row kill matrix from the\n            SCORES alone and confirming each conditioning row is the unique killer of one mutant)\n\n**THE BREACH: the {tiled x bufdepth 8} JOINT CELL was unrowed**, so `(bufdepth == 4 || linear)` is\ntrue in all three rowed cells and scores 9/9 by construction.  Named INDEPENDENTLY by grok, the kimi\nsubstitute and deepseek; kimi wrote the mutant in exactly that form from the packet alone.\n\n**It is the class-closure amendment's own predicted failure**: I repaired cell-by-cell twice, at\nexactly the address each mutant used.  Closed with `rgb tiled64` IN ONE DIFF with the gate fix.\n\n**grok additionally read the GATE against the PROBE** -- which no earlier review had done -- and\nfound `gate_arm.sh` pinning 8 rows and never naming `rgb tiled`.  It would have failed loudly rather\nthan silently, but it would have shipped, and that row would have been uncounted by name.\n\n*** TABLE 6 -- THREE ROWS ARE VOID, AND I VOIDED THEM MYSELF WITH A CHECK THE CARRIER SAYS\nCANNOT FAIL.  2026-09-07. ***\n\nI checked whether the table driver was alive with `ps -ef | grep -c python` and got **0**.  The\ndriver runs `python3` INSIDE WSL, and git-bash `ps -ef` cannot see WSL processes at all -- so that\ncheck returns 0 whether or not anything is running.  **It is the argument-blind process check the\ncarrier already records, reached by a second road: not 'the pattern names a script' but 'the\nprocess is on the other side of the WSL boundary'.**  I read the 0 as a dead run, wrote a resume\ndriver, and launched it into `/tmp/gx505f` **while the original was still building there**.\n\n**THE THREE TELLS I HAD AND MISREAD, in the order they appeared:**\n  1. `M14_compmirror.raw` was 787 bytes and ended at row 6 with no `SGIRGB_RESULT` -- which I read\n     as a truncation.  It was a file **being written**.  The completion-sentinel rule is right and I\n     applied it to the wrong hypothesis: absent sentinel means *not finished*, and *not finished*\n     includes *still going*.\n  2. The `.MUTANT` sentinel said `M14_compmirror` -- **the sentinel was CORRECT and current**, and I\n     treated a live sentinel as a crash residue.\n  3. `grep` reported `binary file matches` on two logs.  That is not a size or a truncation signal;\n     it means the file contains NUL bytes.\n\n**THE EVIDENCE OF THE RACE IS IN THE ARTIFACTS AND IT IS UNAMBIGUOUS:**\n\n    prefix10row.raw   1295 bytes, 339 of them NUL     <- two writers, one seeking past the other\n    M14_compmirror    13:21:47   nullmedium 13:22:13   prefix10row 13:23:04\n\n`nullmedium` is written AFTER `prefix10row` by the driver, so a single run cannot produce that\nordering.  Two runs can.\n\n*** AND M14's LOG IS INTERNALLY IMPOSSIBLE, WHICH IS THE PROOF RATHER THAN THE SUSPICION. ***\n`M14 = cond(bufdepth == 8 || !linear)`.  The `rgb depth64` row drives `MODE_RGB64 = 0x1310`, so\n`bufdepth = 1 << ((0x13) & 3) = 8` and the condition is TRUE -- that row MUST read back the fixed\nvalue.  **It reads back the defect value**, and all four RGB rows report byte-for-byte what\n`M1_verbatim` reports (both 4/10).  A mutant conditioned on a predicate cannot score identically to\nthe unconditional defect unless the binary measured was not the binary intended.\n\n> **VOID: `M14_compmirror`, `prefix10row`, `nullmedium`.**  All three were written inside the race\n> window (13:21:47 onward).  **`base` and `M1`-`M13` STAND**: every one was written before the\n> second driver launched, and `M13_composite` at 13:14:49 -- the mutant this table exists for, at\n> **9/10, KILLED** -- is clear of the window by seven minutes.\n\n**The three void rows are re-measured ALONE, in a FRESH clone, with no concurrent driver.**  Until\nthen table 6 is a 13-of-16 table and the class-closure claim is UNDISCHARGED -- `M14` is the mirror\ncell that closes the width x addressing 2x2, so its absence is exactly the hole pass 3 refused to\nclose the chain over.\n\n*The carrier's remedy was available and I did not take it: **long jobs go to `run_in_background`,\nwhich the harness tracks and notifies on**, so no process check is needed and a second launch\ncannot happen.  The original run WAS launched that way -- I then ignored the harness's own tracking\nand asked the operating system instead.*\n\n> **THE RULE THIS ADDS, and it is narrow: WHEN A HARNESS-TRACKED TASK HAS NOT NOTIFIED, IT IS\n> RUNNING.**  That is what tracking means.  A process check can only ever weaken that -- it cannot\n> strengthen it -- and on this machine it cannot even weaken it honestly, because it is blind to\n> WSL in one direction and to arguments in the other.\n\n*** TABLE 6 COMPLETE AND CLEAN, 2026-09-07, AFTER THE THREE RACED ROWS WERE RE-MEASURED ALONE IN\nA FRESH CLONE (`/tmp/gx505g`). ***\n\n    base_recheck     10/10   <- the FRESHNESS PROOF: the clone reproduces the shipped score\n    M14_compmirror    7/10   KILLED   (the raced run said 4/10)\n    prefix10row       4/10   clause (i), the SHIPPED 10-row script against PRE-FIX source\n    nullmedium        1/10   clause (c), identity rows\n\nEvery log carries **0 NUL bytes** and ends `SGIRGB_DONE`; the restore passed the two-part clearance\nand the sentinel cleared.\n\n**M14's CLEAN VALUE IS INTERNALLY COHERENT WHERE THE RACED ONE WAS NOT, and that is the proof the\nre-measure was necessary rather than merely cautious.**  `M14 = cond(bufdepth == 8 || !linear)` uses\nthe FIXED code exactly where the mode is 64-bit or tiled:\n\n    rgb chan / not corrupt / samemode   LIN_A, depth 4     predicate FALSE -> defect -> RED\n    rgb depth64   0x1310 -> 1<<(0x13&3) = 8                predicate TRUE  -> fixed  -> green\n    rgb tiled     0x0210 -> TLB_A, !linear                 predicate TRUE  -> fixed  -> green\n    rgb tiled64   0x0310 -> both                           predicate TRUE  -> fixed  -> green\n\nThree red, seven green = 7/10.  **The raced log had `rgb depth64` showing the DEFECT value against\nits own TRUE predicate**, which is what exposed the race in the first place.\n\n> **THE WIDTH x ADDRESSING 2x2 IS NOW CLOSED BY A MEASURED MIRROR PAIR** -- `M13_composite`\n> (`bufdepth == 4 || linear`) at 9/10 and `M14_compmirror` (`bufdepth == 8 || !linear`) at 7/10, both\n> KILLED.  That is what pass 3 refused to close the chain without, and it is the class-closure rule\n> discharged rather than argued.\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-08.  IT WAS FIXED BY `#505` AND THE ROW WAS LEFT OPEN -- a stale row, found\nwhile scoping a DIFFERENT round in the same file. ***\n\nThe fix is in the tree and carries its own provenance comment: the GET arm now reads\n`(buf[0] << 24) + (buf[1] << 16) + (buf[2] << 8)`, with a `#505:` block above it recording that it\nused to read `buf[1..3]` and why the PUT arm is the authoritative one.  `CHANGELOG.md` carries a full\n`#505` round block, and the commit is **`7a41b3f`** (\"#505 #506 #507 #508: four test-first rounds,\nthree new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\").\n\n    state    open   -> closed\n    commits  None   -> 7a41b3f\n    witness  None   -> probe:regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py\n\n*** AND `#505`'s CHANGELOG BLOCK CITES A WITNESS PATH THAT DOES NOT EXIST.  Corrected in the same\npass. ***  It names `regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/rgb_witness.py`; that file is absent and, on\nthe evidence of the artifacts directory, never existed under that name.  The witness is\n**`regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`** (27,831 bytes), whose own header opens *\"sgirergbroundtrip --\nRUNG-3 WITNESS for dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel()'s RGB arms\"* and restates the defect exactly.\nSo the witness is real and only the pointer was wrong.\n\n> **A ROW LEFT OPEN OVER A SHIPPED FIX IS THE OVERTAKEN-ROW SHAPE WITHOUT THE OVERTAKING.**  The\n> queue said a med defect was live in a file three rounds were actively editing.  I read this row\n> while deciding the scope of the `sgirandomcolour` round, took its title at face value, and started\n> designing around a defect that was already gone -- *which is the second time this session a stale\n> record nearly shaped a round*.\n\n*Found by reading the SOURCE while assembling a cluster brief, not by any check.  Nothing in the\nharness compares a row's open/closed state against the presence of its own fix in the tree, and this\nis not an argument that something should: the honest position is that it was luck, and the cheap\ndefence is the one that worked -- open the file before believing a row's title.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:32522:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) and verified:\n\n    for (dev = 0; dev < 21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\n0x800 is a plain int (2^11).  At dev == 20 the expression is 2^31, which overflows a signed 32-bit int --\n**undefined behaviour** in C99, not merely implementation-defined.\n\n**The irony worth recording: this loop is the file's own PRECEDENT for graceful degradation.**  Its\n`dev >= 21' fallback has a commented-out fatal()/exit(1) and executes `dev = 0;' instead -- the\ndelete-the-check-and-substitute-a-safe-default disposition that [[lcabadval]] cites as a transferable\nin-tree warrant.  **The safe path contains UB.**\n\nLOW: `0x800U << dev' fixes it, and on every compiler this project targets the emitted code is already what\nwas intended.  Recorded because a UB shift is exactly what a sanitiser build exists to catch, and\nbuild-asan/ is kept clean -- **so the first question is whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not,\nwhy not.**\n\n*** THE FILED QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER COMES WITH A FREE VEHICLE: THE PROBE SHIPPED FOR\n[[lcaconfbus1kill]] ALREADY EVALUATES THIS EXPRESSION ON EVERY RUN. ***\n\nThis row asks *\"whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not, why not.\"*  Both halves settled\nby reading, 2026-09-07.\n\n**UBSAN IS PRESENT.**  `build-asan/Makefile:9` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`.\n\n**BUT THE SWEEP CANNOT REACH THIS SITE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` runs, per machine type and subtype:\n\n    timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 \"$bin\" -V -E \"$t\" -e \"$s\" \"$DUMMY\" </dev/null\n\nThat is a **CONSTRUCTION** sweep -- it builds each machine and lets it start.  The shift lives in\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s device-scan loop, which runs only when a guest performs a PCI\nCONFIG-SPACE ACCESS.  **Constructing `alphabook1` never executes that loop**, so no amount of\nsweeping flags it.  *This is the witness-ladder distinction one level down: rung 2 (construction)\ncannot reach an access-path defect, which is exactly what the ladder says about registration\nproving presence and not reachability.*\n\n*** AND THE VEHICLE ALREADY EXISTS, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH ACTING ON. ***\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 as the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) drives a\nguest `ldq` into the PCI config window with `ioc_conf == 0`.  That takes the `bus == 0` branch:\n\n    for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\nIts control read uses `relative_addr == 0`, so `tag = (relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff` is **0**, the\n`if` is never true, and **the loop runs all 21 iterations** -- evaluating `0x800 << 20` at the last\none.  `0x800` is `2^11` and a plain `int`, so `0x800 << 20` is `2^31`: **signed overflow, C99\n6.5.7p4, undefined behaviour, on every single run of that probe.**\n\n> **SO THE MEASUREMENT IS: run the existing `lca_confbus_probe.py` against `build-asan/gxemul` and\n> read stderr for a UBSAN shift diagnostic.  No new probe, no new row, no new apparatus.**  The\n> expected diagnostic names `dev_lca.c` and a left-shift of a signed value.\n\n**NOT RUN YET** -- a probe run must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.  Queued.\n\n**IF IT FIRES**, this row gains a measured witness and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) can be\ngraded properly.  **IF IT DOES NOT**, that is itself a finding about\n[[ubsanshiftlane]] -- it would mean the ASAN build is not reporting a shift UB that is provably\nevaluated, and the whole proposed UBSAN differential lane rests on an assumption that does not hold.\n**Either outcome is worth the one run**, which is the argument for doing it before designing the lane.\n\n*Found while answering this row's own filed question rather than by looking for it -- and note the\nirony the row already recorded: this loop is the file's OWN graceful-degradation precedent, cited by\n[[lcabadval]] as a transferable warrant.  The safe path contains the UB.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE UBSAN DIAGNOSTIC FIRES, NAMING THE FILE, LINE, COLUMN, VALUE AND\nSHIFT COUNT.  Artifact: `regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n    GXemul> step 5\n    fffffc0001040000:  243f0102    ldah  t0,0x102\n    fffffc0001040004:  a4410010    ldq   t1,16(t0)\n    fffffc0001040008:  a4810000    ldq   t3,0(t0)\n    fffffc000104000c:  a4210008    ldq   t0,8(t0)\n    fffffc0001040010:  a4620000    ldq   t2,0(t1)\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n    GXemul> print t2\n    0xffffffffffffffff\n\n**`2048` is `0x800`; `by 20 places` is the final loop iteration; `dev_lca.c:125` is exactly\n`if (tag & (0x800 << dev))`.**  The diagnostic lands on the guest `ldq t2,0(t1)` -- a real guest\ninstruction through real address decode on an unmodified `alphabook1`, with the empty-slot signature\n`0xffffffffffffffff` printed immediately after.\n\n**NO NEW APPARATUS WAS BUILT.**  This is `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, shipped earlier the same day\nas the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]], run against `build-asan/gxemul` instead of `build/gxemul`.\nThe control read takes the `bus == 0` branch with `tag == 0`, so the scan loop runs all 21\niterations and reaches the overflow every time.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST MEASUREMENT WAS A FALSE NEGATIVE OF MY OWN MAKING, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE\nRESULT. ***  Run 1 reported NO diagnostic and I nearly recorded that as a finding against\n[[ubsanshiftlane]].  The cause: **I grepped the PROBE's stdout, and a UBSAN message goes to the\nEMULATOR's stderr**, which lands in the probe's internal pty buffer and is never echoed.  That is\n*exactly* the defect I had fixed hours earlier for the `bus attrib` row -- a green row whose evidence\nnever reaches the artifact -- recurring one level out, on the harness rather than the row.\n\n> **A PROBE ONLY SHOWS YOU WHAT IT CHOOSES TO PRINT.  When you are looking for output the probe was\n> not written to surface, dump the buffer -- do not grep the summary.**  Re-run from a frozen COPY\n> in `/tmp` (the shipped probe is not edited for a measurement) with the full buffer echoed, and the\n> diagnostic was in the first ten lines.\n\n*The wrong answer REASSURED, which is this project's recurring signature: \"no diagnostic\" is exactly\nwhat a clean build looks like.*\n\n**THIS ROW NOW HAS A MEASURED WITNESS** and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) is gradeable.\nThe repair is COMPLETE for this site: `0x800U` makes the operand unsigned, so `2048U << 20` is\n`0x80000000U`, well inside `unsigned int`, and the `tag &` comparison is unaffected because `tag` is\nalready a wider unsigned quantity.\n\n**SEVERITY STAYS LOW and that is deliberate**: the overflow is UB by the language, the emitted code\non every compiler this project targets is what was intended, and no wrong guest-visible value has\nbeen measured.  What changed is that it is now WITNESSED rather than argued.\n\n*** THE FIX IS DESIGNED AND EXECUTABLE.  Written out here so the next session can run it rather than\nre-derive it -- the round is small and every input is now in hand. ***\n\n**THE EDIT, one token:**\n\n    -\tif (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n    +\tif (tag & (0x800U << dev))\t/*  #NNN  */\n\n`0x800U` makes the operand `unsigned int`, so `2048U << 20` is `0x80000000U` -- well inside\n`unsigned int` -- and no other behaviour moves: `tag` is already a wider unsigned quantity, so the\n`&` and the truth value are unchanged at every one of the 21 iterations.  **The emitted code on\nevery compiler this project targets is what was already intended**, which is why the severity is LOW\neven though the language calls it undefined.\n\n*** THE TEST-FIRST ORDER IS ALREADY SATISFIED, WHICH IS UNUSUAL AND WORTH NOTING. ***  The witness\nexists and is RED on the committed build: running `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` against\n`build-asan/gxemul` emits\n\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n\nSo the reproduce-before-you-edit step is DONE and preserved\n(`regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`).  The post-fix half is: the same command,\nsame binary path, and **the diagnostic must be ABSENT**.  Same script, opposite outcome -- which is\nthe ladder's clause (i) exactly.\n\n**WHAT THE ROUND OWES, and the one real decision in it:**\n\nThe two-direction control above is a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL, and this project has that filed as its own\nround ([[ubsanshiftlane]]) because it is a NEW MEASUREMENT LANE -- a second binary and a STDERR\noracle rather than a value oracle.  **So the honest choice is:**\n\n* **(a) ship the fix with the UBSAN differential as its detector**, which means building at least a\n  minimal version of the lane in this round -- and a lane added inside a fix round is a lane nobody\n  reviewed, which is the exact objection `ubsanshiftlane` was filed to avoid; or\n* **(b) ship the fix with a SOURCE PIN** -- a check asserting the expression is `0x800U` -- and\n  leave the differential to its own round.  `#510`'s own product comments already name a source pin\n  as the legitimate alternative to UBSAN for precisely this class (\"No row can kill a `1U' -> `1'\n  regression; UBSAN or a source pin can\").\n\n**(b) is in scope and (a) is not**, on this project's own stopping rule: the fix is a one-token\ncorrectness repair, and expanding it into a new measurement lane is the scope creep the rule exists\nto refuse.  *Recorded as a reasoned choice rather than a preference, so a reviewer can disagree with\nthe reason.*\n\n**COST: a build.**  The edit must propagate to all four trees and `build-asan` must be REBUILT for\nthe post-fix measurement, which is the only expensive part and is why this was not executed the\nmoment it was designed -- a rebuild must not overlap a running seat.\n\n**AND THE POST-FIX RUN IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIX.**  If the diagnostic does NOT\ndisappear, the repair is wrong or the site is not the one being reported, and that is a finding\nabout the measurement rather than a formality.  Predict-then-measure: it should vanish, and nothing\nelse in the probe's four rows should move (3/4 before, 3/4 after -- the `bus kill` row is a\ndifferent defect and stays RED).\n\n*** FIXED AND MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, 2026-09-07, as `#511`. ***\n\n    HEAD    1 diagnostic     ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw\n    FIXED   0 diagnostics    ubsan_FIXED_20260907.raw\n    control  build/ scores 3/4 before AND after, same three rows green, `bus kill' still RED\n             (a different defect) -- NOTHING ELSE MOVED\n\n**The pre-registered prediction held exactly**: the diagnostic vanished and no other row moved.  All\nfour trees cleared two-part after the propagate (`est/`: source tree, part 2 N/A).\n\n**The detector is the differential itself and it is NOT GATE-WIRED**, which the CHANGELOG block says\nin those words.  A source pin was considered and rejected: it would test the TEXT while this round\nhas the BEHAVIOUR.  Gate-wiring is [[ubsanshiftlane]], deliberately separate.\n\n*** THE SIBLING QUESTION IS ANSWERED BEFORE THE PANEL ANSWERS IT, AND THE RESULT IS A CLEAN\nABSENCE -- RECORDED WITH ITS WARRANT SO IT IS FALSIFIABLE. ***\n\n`#511`'s own pass-2 brief asks (Q5) whether a sibling of this shape was missed.  Censused:\n\n    signed-constant  <<  VARIABLE, tree-wide                       317 sites\n    of those, literal > 1 AND headroom <= 24                          2 sites\n\n**317 IS A CORRELATE, NOT THE CLASS.**  `1 << x` with 31 places of headroom is the ordinary bitmask\nidiom in C and is a defect only if `x` can reach 31.  The `#511` SHAPE is distinctive: a literal\nLARGER than 1 (so little headroom) shifted by a variable that a loop can drive to the overflow point.\nFiltering on that leaves TWO:\n\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:772   tmp &= 0x0fff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 20\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:774   tmp &= 0xffff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 16\n\n**BOTH ARE SAFE, and the warrant is a compile-time constant:**\n\n    src/include/cop0.h:85    #define PAGEMASK_SHIFT   13\n    0x0fff << 13 = 0x01ffe000       0xffff << 13 = 0x1fffe000       INT_MAX = 0x7fffffff\n\n`PAGEMASK_SHIFT` is a MACRO, not a runtime variable, so the compiler folds the whole expression and\nthere is no shift to overflow.  **My regex matched an identifier and could not tell a macro from a\nvariable** -- which is why the two hits needed reading rather than counting.\n\n> **[THIS ABSENCE RECORD IS FALSE.  See the correction at the end of this note -- the grok pass-2\n> seat found TWO siblings my filter had hidden.]**  ~~no other site in the tree has a signed literal > 1 shifted by a value that can reach 2^31.~~  THE WARRANT IS\n> `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` AND ITS BEING A COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT.**  If either changes -- the value\n> raised past 15, or the macro replaced by a variable -- the two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites become\n> live and this record expires.\n\n*A near-miss worth keeping: my first attempt grepped `define PAGEMASK_SHIFT` in `src/` and returned\nNOTHING, and I had already computed the arithmetic assuming 13.  Zero hits is not evidence, and the\nnumber I was about to rely on was a guess.  It IS 13 -- found in `src/include/cop0.h` on a broader\nsearch -- but the conclusion was reached before the fact was.*\n\n*** THE ABSENCE RECORD ABOVE IS FALSE, AND MY OWN FILTER IS WHAT MADE IT SO.  The grok pass-2 seat\nfound TWO SIBLINGS, both verified here. ***\n\ngrok's verdict on `#511` closes with: *\"Two other PCI IDSEL scanners still evaluate `1 << 31`.\"*\nVerified by reading:\n\n    dev_uninorth.c:70   for (dev=11; dev<32; dev++)\n                 :71       if (idata & (1 << dev))            <- dev REACHES 31\n    dev_v3.c:164        for (dev=24; dev<32; dev++)\n            :165           if (relative_addr & (1 << dev))    <- dev REACHES 31\n\n    1 << 31 = 0x80000000 = 2147483648    INT_MAX = 2147483647    OVERFLOWS\n\n**Both are the IDENTICAL SHAPE to `#511`** -- a signed literal shifted by a loop variable the loop\nbound drives to the overflow point -- and both loops explicitly run to `dev < 32`, so 31 is reached\nwhenever no earlier bit matches.\n\n*** THE FILTER THAT HID THEM WAS MINE, AND IT WAS EXACTLY BACKWARDS. ***  My census excluded\n`if v <= 1: continue`, reasoning that ``1 << x`` is the ordinary bitmask idiom in C and only a\ndefect if `x` reaches 31.  **That reasoning is right and the exclusion is wrong**: `1` has the MOST\nheadroom (31 places), which makes it the ONLY literal that overflows at the maximum shift a\n`< 32` loop can produce.  I filtered on \"little headroom = suspicious\" and thereby removed the\nsingle literal whose headroom exactly matches the commonest loop bound in the tree.\n\n> **AN ABSENCE RECORD IS ONLY AS GOOD AS THE FILTER THAT PRODUCED IT, AND MY WARRANT NAMED THE\n> WRONG THING.**  I recorded the warrant as `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` -- a fact about the two sites the\n> filter DID surface -- when the load-bearing assumption was the FILTER ITSELF.  A warrant should\n> name what would falsify the record, and what falsified it was the exclusion, not the constant.\n\n**The `PAGEMASK_SHIFT` half stands**: those two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites really are safe, for the\nreason given.  What is retracted is the universal claim that nothing else in the tree has the shape.\n\n*Filed to [[shiftubcensus]], which is the row for this class and was already marked HONESTLY\nPARTIAL.  Recorded here too because THIS row is where the false absence was written.*\n\n*** codex, `#511` PASS 2, CHALLENGES THE \"GRACEFUL DEGRADATION PRECEDENT\" THAT BOTH LCA ROWS LEAN\nON. ***\n\n*\"`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s fallback selects device zero and forwards the access through\n`bus_pci_setaddr`.  **That is an ALIASING DECISION, not by itself proof of harmless degradation.**\nThe comment's 'transferable warrant' needs its own justification; the shift repair does not establish\nit.\"*\n\nThe `dev >= 21` arm -- commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted -- has been\ncited repeatedly here, including by [[lcabadval]], as the file's own in-tree warrant for deleting a\nhost kill.  **codex's point is that substituting device ZERO is not a decline: it silently redirects\nthe access to a DIFFERENT DEVICE.**  Whether that is harmless is a separate question nobody has\nasked, and \"someone already did it in this file\" is not the same as \"it is right\".\n\n> **A TRANSFERRED WARRANT NEEDS ITS OWN JUSTIFICATION, NOT JUST A PRECEDENT.**  This is the\n> option-set rule's own caution turned around: grepping the fork for an existing idiom finds what the\n> tree DOES, which is evidence about practice and not about correctness.\n\n*Not resolved here.  Recorded on both rows because both cite it, and any round that leans on it owes\nthe justification codex is asking for.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34382:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, split out of [[shiftubcensus]] item (A) because it is the only member of that\ncensus with a **GUEST-VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE** rather than a language-level one, and it therefore owes a\nwitness the others do not.\n\n**THE SITE**, `dev_uninorth.c`, in the PCI address-register write path:\n\n    :56    uint64_t idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len | MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN);\n    ...\n    :60    d->cur_addr = idata;\n    :70    for (dev=11; dev<32; dev++)\n    :71        if (idata & (1 << dev))\n    :72            break;\n\n**`idata` is `uint64_t` and `1 << dev` is an `int`.**  At `dev == 31` that is `0x80000000`, which as\na signed `int` is NEGATIVE, so converting it for the `&` against a `uint64_t` **SIGN-EXTENDS it to\n`0xffffffff80000000`**.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE IF `idata` CARRIES ANY BIT IN 32..63: the loop matches at `dev == 31` when no\n> device bit is set there, and the scan selects the WRONG DEVICE.**  The repair (`1U << dev`) is\n> therefore NOT behaviour-preserving here -- unlike its sibling in `dev_v3.c`, where the operand's\n> high bits are provably zero and the same edit changes nothing.\n\n**REACHABLE AS A DEVICE:** `machine_macppc.c:68` calls\n`dev_uninorth_init(machine, machine->memory, 0xe2000000, ...)`, and `dev_uninorth.c:141` registers\n`\"uninorth_pci_addr\"` at `addr + 0x800000` = **0xe2800000**.  macppc is a supported machine here.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, AND IT IS THE WHOLE QUESTION: CAN `idata` ACTUALLY CARRY BITS 32..63? ***\n`memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len | MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN)` returns a value whose width follows the\nACCESS WIDTH.  On a 4-byte store -- the ordinary way a guest writes a PCI config address -- the\nresult cannot exceed 32 bits, the sign-extended ones meet only zeros, and **nothing observable\nhappens**.  The defect needs an **8-byte** access to that register.\n\n**So the row is HELD at the reachability question, not at the arithmetic.**  A codex analysis is out\non exactly this (`regress/artifacts/shiftubcensus/UNINORTH_brief.md`), asking: what does\n`memory_readmax64` return per width; what widths reach this handler on macppc; and -- the premise I\nam least sure of -- whether `MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN`, which is OR'd into the `len` PARAMETER, can\nperturb the length itself rather than only the byte order.\n\n**SEVERITY DELIBERATELY LEFT AT `med` PENDING THAT ANSWER.**  If an 8-byte access reaches the\nhandler this is a guest-drivable mis-decode on a supported machine and should be HIGH; if it cannot,\nthis collapses into the same language-level hygiene as the other two census members and should be\nLOW.  *Grading it before the answer would be picking the number I prefer.*\n\n**THE REPAIR IS ONE TOKEN EITHER WAY** (`1U << dev`), and it is correct under both outcomes -- the\nquestion is only what the round may CLAIM, and whether it owes a witness.\n\n*** MY SHAKIEST PREMISE IS ANSWERED BY READING, AND IT ANSWERS THE REASSURING WAY -- SO THE ROW\nNARROWS TO EXACTLY ONE QUESTION. ***\n\nI flagged as least certain whether `MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN`, OR'd into the **`len` parameter**, could\nperturb the LENGTH rather than only the byte order.  `src/core/memory.c`:\n\n    #define MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN   128           (memory.h:98)\n\n    uint64_t memory_readmax64(struct cpu *cpu, unsigned char *buf, int len)\n    {\n            int i, byte_order = cpu->byte_order;\n            uint64_t x = 0;\n\n            if (len & MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN) {\n                    len &= ~MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN;      <- EXPLICITLY STRIPPED\n                    byte_order = EMUL_LITTLE_ENDIAN;\n            }\n            ... for (i=0; i<len; i++) { x <<= 8; x |= buf[i]; }\n\n**The flag is stripped from `len` before it is used as a count, so it cannot change the length.**\nAnd the two cannot collide in practice: the flag is 128 while access widths are 1..8.\n\n**WHAT THAT LEAVES, and it is now the WHOLE question:** `x` accumulates exactly `len` bytes, so\n\n    len == 4  ->  x <= 0xffffffff, bits 32..63 are ZERO  ->  the sign-extended ones meet only\n                  zeros and NOTHING OBSERVABLE HAPPENS\n    len == 8  ->  x can carry bits 32..63                ->  the spurious dev==31 match is REAL\n\n> **So the row turns entirely on: can a guest issue an 8-BYTE access to `0xe2800000` on macppc, and\n> does it reach this handler with `len == 8` intact?**  Nothing else matters -- not the endianness\n> flag, not the arithmetic, both of which are now settled.\n\n*Recorded because narrowing a question is worth as much as answering one, and because this half was\nsettled by reading two functions while a seat works the other half.  The codex analysis in flight is\nasked the same thing independently; if it disagrees about the stripping, the disagreement is\ncheckable in four lines.*\n\n*** ANSWERED: **NO**.  THE ROW IS DOWNGRADED med -> low, AND THE INVESTIGATION IS WHY THE SEVERITY\nWAS LEFT UNGRADED IN THE FIRST PLACE. ***\n\ncodex: *\"The macppc guest-store paths cannot place bits 32..63 in this register's `idata` or\n`cur_addr`: the dispatcher clamps the four-byte address window before calling the handler.\n`MEM_PCI_LITTLE_ENDIAN` preserves these access widths.\"*  **Both halves verified here rather than\naccepted:**\n\n    dev_uninorth.c:141-142   memory_device_register(mem, \"uninorth_pci_addr\", addr + 0x800000,\n                                 4, dev_uninorth_addr_access, d, DM_DEFAULT, NULL);\n                                 ^-- REGISTERED LENGTH 4\n\n    memory_rw.c:495          if (paddr + len > mem->devices[i].length)   <- truncates, with the\n                                 fork's own #91/#93/#94 per-device note above it\n\nSo an 8-byte guest store to `0xe2800000` is **truncated to 4 bytes before the handler runs**.\n`memory_readmax64` then accumulates at most 4 bytes, `idata <= 0xffffffff`, bits 32..63 are ZERO,\nand the sign-extended ones in `0xffffffff80000000` meet only zeros.  **Nothing observable happens.**\n\n> **SO THIS COLLAPSES INTO THE SAME LANGUAGE-LEVEL HYGIENE AS THE OTHER TWO [[shiftubcensus]]\n> MEMBERS.**  The shift is still UB and still worth the one-token repair; what is retracted is the\n> claim that it mis-decodes a device.\n\n*** THE PROCESS POINT IS THE VALUE HERE. ***  The arithmetic was right, the device really is\ninstantiated on a supported machine, and the sign-extension really would select the wrong device --\n**every step held except reachability, and reachability is what decided it.**  Had the row been\ngraded HIGH on the arithmetic alone it would have carried a guest-visible claim that is false, and a\nfix round would have been designed around a witness that cannot exist.  *Leaving the number blank\ncost one codex invocation and bought a correct grade.*\n\n**A DETAIL WORTH KEEPING:** the sibling `uninorth_pci_data` port at `+0xc00000` IS registered with\nlength **8** (`:143-144`).  So the width concern is not absurd in general for this device -- it is\nsimply wrong for the ADDRESS port, which is the one with the shift.  A future reader tempted to\ngeneralise \"uninorth clamps to 4\" should not."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34584:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-08 as `#512`'s own declared floor breach.  **The round graded it a BREACH rather than clause-(e) debt on purpose**: `assert = 0` is the repair's effect, so nulling it is (d)'s canonical form and sits inside the closure.  THE SHAPE OF THE REPAIR: breakpoint + `continue` so host-timer callbacks run, T1 enabled with a small compare so a timer interrupt goes pending, MIEN and C040 both set, and the oracle is the `debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\")` at the foot of reassert_interrupts() reading `no assert`.  *That channel IS visible in a stepped capture -- what cannot vary is the STATE, because no guest store can set an ICR INT bit.*\n\n*** THE ORACLE THIS ROW WAS FILED WITH IS WRONG, AND THE codex SEAT HAD ALREADY SAID SO IN AN ANSWER\nI HAD RECORDED AS \"not yet fully mined\".  Corrected 2026-09-08, VERIFIED BY LINE ORDER. ***\n\nThe row proposed reading `debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\")` for `no assert`.  **That\nmessage EXECUTES BEFORE BOTH SUPPRESSIONS:**\n\n    :334   debug(\"[ pcc2: IPL = %i, MASK = %i => %s ]\" ...)   <-- prints `assert' HERE\n    :339   if (!(GENCTL & PCC2_MIEN))       assert = 0;\n    :364   if (GENCTL & PCC2_C040)        { ... assert = 0; }\n    :374   if (assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT ... else INTERRUPT_DEASSERT\n\nSo it reports the PRE-suppression value and is **green under exactly the mutant it would be chosen\nto catch**.  codex: *\"do not use the existing `IPL ... => ASSERT' debug message as the line-state\noracle: it executes before MIEN suppression and before the proposed C040 suppression.  Observe the\ninterrupt callback/state.\"*\n\n> **THE CORRECT ORACLE IS THE INTERRUPT CALLBACK/STATE, not the diagnostic.**  A free-running probe\n> is still required for the STATE (an ICR INT bit cannot be set by a guest store), but the CHANNEL\n> changes: observe delivery, or instrument the callback -- never the pre-suppression debug line.\n\n*This is the channel-line check failing on a row I wrote while quoting the channel-line check.  A\nrow whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous by construction -- and here\nthe channel was not merely invariant, it was measured at the wrong POINT IN THE FUNCTION.*\n\n*** THE \"NEEDS A FREE-RUNNING PROBE\" WARRANT IS WRONG.  Refuted at pass 2 by grok and VERIFIED here\n2026-09-08. ***\n\n`pcctwo_interrupt_common()` ORs `PCC2_IRQ_INT` onto SCSIICR/SCCTX/SCCRX when those lines assert, and\n`dev_clmpcc.c:88-89` calls `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq_scc_tx)` **synchronously inside a guest store to\nCLMPCC_REG_IER**; `machine_mvme88k.c` constructs clmpcc with its irq wired to pcc2.  **So the state\nis reachable while single-stepping** -- `st.b` IEN|IL to SCCTX, `st.b` to the clmpcc IER, `st.b`\n`C040|MIEN` (0x06) to GENCTL.\n\n**THIS ROW IS THEREFORE AN IN-ROUND L2 OBLIGATION, NOT A FILED ONE**, and `pcc2c040kill` has been\nreopened accordingly.  The remaining difficulty is the ORACLE, not the state: the `debug()` line is\nbefore both suppressions, so the observable must be the interrupt line itself -- `INTERRUPT_ASSERT`\n/ `cpu->cd.m88k.irq_asserted` / guest delivery.\n\n*Two wrong reasons in a row on this same question -- first \"the channel is invisible\" (false: it\nprints while stepping), then \"the state is unreachable\" (false: another device's store sets it).\nThe conclusion \"no row today\" was right both times and neither reason was.*\n\n*** THE VEHICLE EXISTS AND IS SHIPPED.  `regress/m88k_exc_probe.py` IS ALREADY AN m88k FREE-RUNNING\nEXCEPTION-DELIVERY PROBE.  Found 2026-09-08 by looking instead of reasoning. ***\n\nIts own header describes the shape this row needs, verbatim:\n\n> *\"seeded with `rN=`, a `br .` (c0000000) planted at every vector entry so a delivered exception\n> spins rather than executing zeros, `breakpoint add <8*vector>`, `continue`, then `reg` and\n> `reg 0,0`.\"*\n\n**So the repair is a COPY of a proven in-tree shape, not a new capability.**  That matters for the\ndisposition: \"needs a different probe shape\" sounded like a capability gap and is really a\nhalf-day's careful work against an existing template.\n\n**THE ORACLE, now that both halves are settled:**\n\n    STATE   (stepped-reachable, per grok/pass 2) st.b IEN|IL -> SCCTX; st.b -> CLMPCC_REG_IER,\n            whose handler calls INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq_scc_tx) synchronously inside the store;\n            pcctwo_interrupt_common() then ORs PCC2_IRQ_INT into SCCTX.\n    ORACLE  (needs FREE-RUNNING) the m88k INTERRUPT vector entry.  Plant `br .' there,\n            `breakpoint add', `continue'.  Breakpoint FIRES  = the line asserted = the mutant.\n            Loop survives to timeout                          = suppressed  = the fix.\n\n> **WHY THE ORACLE STILL NEEDS FREE-RUNNING EVEN THOUGH THE STATE DOES NOT** -- and this refines\n> grok's *\"the obstacle is the oracle, not the state\"* rather than contradicting it.  The only\n> difference between the fix and the mutant is `INTERRUPT_ASSERT` versus `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` on\n> `d->cpu_irq`; `cpu->cd.m88k.irq_asserted` is a HOST-side field with no guest-readable reflection,\n> so the sole guest-visible consequence is DELIVERY -- and `cpu_dyntrans.c` suppresses interrupt\n> dispatch while single-stepping by design (*\"Do not cause interrupts while single-stepping\"*).\n> **State: stepped.  Delivery: free-running.  Both facts are needed and neither alone settles it.**\n\n*Note the adjacency to `m88kexcdetectorl2`, which is a HIGH row about that same probe.  These are\nseparate rows and must not be merged -- same vehicle, different defect, different oracle.*\n\n*** THE DESIGN REVIEW CAUGHT TWO DEFECTS BEFORE A LINE WAS WRITTEN, AND THE FIRST IS THAT THE ROW\nWOULD HAVE PASSED ITS OWN MUTANT.  codex, 2026-09-08, VERDICT: BUILD-WITH-REVISIONS. ***\n\n### 1.  *** THE PROPOSED DEFECT ROW STILL PASSES THE `return` MUTANT. ***\n\n**GENCTL resets to ZERO**, so MIEN is clear throughout the setup and every completed recalculation\nDEASSERTS the CPU line.  At the final GENCTL write:\n\n    shipped repair            deasserts the line          -> no interrupt   PASS\n    `assert = 0' DELETED      asserts the line            -> interrupt      caught\n    `assert = 0' -> `return'  leaves the line UNCHANGED   -> no interrupt   *** PASSES ***\n\n> *\"Deleting the assignment PERMITS ASSERTION; returning PRESERVES THE INCOMING LINE STATE.  The\n> callbacks simply assign `irq_asserted = 1` or `0`; returning invokes neither.\"*\n\n**My §3 treated the two mutants as interchangeable.  They are not**, and the row was built around the\none that happens to be easier to catch.\n\n**THE FIX IS A PRECONDITION, not a new oracle: write MIEN-ONLY GENCTL IN BOTH ROWS FIRST**, so the\nCPU line is already ASSERTED before the operation under test.  Then `return` leaves it asserted, the\ninterrupt is delivered, and the row reddens.\n\n### 2.  *** THE ORACLE WAS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL.  VERIFIED IN THE CODE. ***\n\n`session()`'s `rd()` tests `until in buf` **against the ENTIRE ACCUMULATED TRANSCRIPT**, at the top\nof its loop -- and `alive = rd(settle)` has already drained the boot banner, which ends at a\n`GXemul>` prompt.  So `(\"continue\", grace, \"GXemul>\")` returns **True with ZERO READS**.  codex\nexercised the extracted reader in memory and got exactly that.\n\n**And `how == \"no-breakpoint\"` is not the negative oracle I took it for**: it establishes a MISSING\nTOKEN, not successful execution without delivery.  The runner deliberately preserves that status\nthrough forced termination and unreaped outcomes, *\"so a C040-specific hang or unexpected guest halt\ncan satisfy a timeout-only negative oracle.\"*\n\n### 3.  THE REVISED DESIGN (to build)\n\n1. Fresh machine per row; perform setup while STEPPING.\n2. Establish the pending SCC TX request, then write **MIEN-only GENCTL in BOTH rows** -- this is the\n   precondition from §1.  *Do not rewrite SCCTX afterwards: its write arm CLEARS INT.*\n3. Final GENCTL write: MIEN-only for the control, MIEN|C040 for the defect row.\n4. `continue` into a FINITE guest path with a **separate normal-completion breakpoint**.  The\n   control must stop at the interrupt entry; the defect row must reach NORMAL COMPLETION without\n   hitting it.  **That gives the negative case affirmative evidence instead of a timeout.**\n5. Attribute stops by FRESH breakpoint evidence and PC.  **Treat timeout, unexpected stop or death\n   as FAILURE**, never as the negative result.\n\n### 4.  EVERY HARDWARE PREMISE CONFIRMED, one with a better warrant than I had\n\n* **PSR `0x80000000`** keeps supervisor mode, clears IND and SFRZ, preserves endianness; the\n  dispatch condition is `irq_asserted && !(PSR & IND)` outside single-stepping.  **It also checked\n  my `stcr` word against the decoder** -- it does select `stcr r5,cr1`.  *That is the\n  hand-assembled-encoding trap closed by someone other than its author.*\n* **VBR = 0 is right, and for a better reason than mine:** `mvmeprom.c:59` EXPLICITLY sets it to\n  zero, rather than it being an assumed reset value.\n* **Entry `0x8`**: interrupt is vector 1 and entry sets PC to `VBR + 8 * vector`.\n* **Source and priority:** SCCTX takes `0x13`; a qualifying IER write asserts SCC TX synchronously,\n  making it `0x33`; recalculation selects IPL 3; MASK starts at 0, so the comparison succeeds.\n\n### 5.  TWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN Q2 EXPECTATIONS\n\n* **\"Suppression by another route\" is NOT inherently a defect.**  *\"A behaviorally equivalent\n  implementation should pass.  The defect is violated behavior or collateral state changes, not\n  failure to use the local variable named `assert`.\"*\n* **My \"both rows fail to deliver\" escape is not a green pair** -- `(no interrupt, no interrupt)`\n  FAILS the positive control.  It becomes an escape only if the grader omits or neutralises that\n  control failure, which is an argument for asserting the control's outcome explicitly rather than\n  treating it as informational.\n\n**Residual classes it names as still uncovered by a delivery-only pair:** C040 suppression that\ncorrupts MASK/MIEN/pending state or IPL bookkeeping; suppression that stays stuck after C040 clears\n(needs a RECOVERY observation); and suppression that works only for the tested source, priority or\nfirst invocation.\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-08.  ALL THREE ESCAPING MUTANTS ARE KILLED. ***\n\n    BASE            11/11\n    M_d1_noassert    9/10   `assert = 0' DELETED        -- was 7/7 (a CLEAN SWEEP)   KILLED\n    M_return         9/10   `assert = 0' -> `return;'   -- the one-token pass-2 mutant KILLED\n    M_latchinside   10/11   `assert = 0' INSIDE the latch -- was 10/10               KILLED\n\n**The rows, and what each buys:**\n\n    irq pending     SCCTX reads 0x33 after the clmpcc IER store -- LIVENESS.  Without it, \"no\n                    interrupt\" is indistinguishable from \"no pending request was ever created\".\n    irq live        C040 CLEAR: the guest REACHES the interrupt vector (pc = 0x8).  POSITIVE\n                    CONTROL, and it is what makes the negative case meaningful.\n    c040 noassert   C040 SET on an ALREADY-ASSERTED line: reaches COMPLETION (pc = 0x1001c).\n    c040 noassert2  the SECOND C040 encounter, latch already set: also reaches completion.\n\n*** TWO DESIGN CORRECTIONS MADE THIS WORK, AND NEITHER WAS MINE. ***\n\n1. **The precondition.**  My first design wrote C040 into a RESET GENCTL, so MIEN was clear, the\n   line was already deasserted, and `return` -- which merely preserves the incoming line state --\n   would have PASSED.  Writing MIEN-only GENCTL FIRST, in BOTH rows, is what makes it catchable.\n   *codex, design review, before a line was written.*\n2. **The encounter axis.**  `assert = 0` moved inside the latch suppresses correctly on the FIRST\n   encounter and skips it on every later one, so a single-encounter row scores 10/10 against it.\n   **Closing the axis with one repeated write beats adding a row per mutant** -- the class-closure\n   rule rather than pass-per-instance.  *Predicted by codex on pass 1, measured here.*\n\n**AND THE NEGATIVE CASE IS AFFIRMATIVE, NOT A TIMEOUT.**  Both outcomes stop at a breakpoint and the\ndiscriminator is WHICH PC.  The obvious oracles were both unusable: the `debug()` line executes\nBEFORE the suppressions, and `(\"continue\", grace, \"GXemul>\")` returns True with ZERO READS because\n`rd()` matches against the whole accumulated buffer, which already holds a prompt.\n\n**RESIDUAL, disclosed rather than implied:** these four rows establish DELIVERY under one source, one\npriority and two encounters.  They do not cover C040 suppression that corrupts MASK/MIEN/pending\nstate, suppression that stays stuck after C040 CLEARS (a recovery observation), or other sources and\npriorities.  *And per codex: suppression by a different implementation route is NOT a defect -- \"the\ndefect is violated behavior or collateral state changes, not failure to use the local variable named\n`assert`.\"*\n\n*** COMMITTED AS `9fa07fe`, inside `#512`.  This row was FILED as coverage debt and CLOSED IN-ROUND\ninstead, which is what pass 2 required. ***  It never became a separate round: the four rows it\nspecified ship as part of the detector they were owed to."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34762:   "title": "wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py -- a single-leg mode plus a pass/fail contract for the ONE WDC two-leg differential that is genuinely infrastructure-blocked: it imports none of wdc_altoffset_probe, carries its own pty/Emu, ties its expectations to DISK CONTENT (needs a POISONED image the battery does not build), and adds an endianness axis (-e maltabe).  RETITLED 2026-09-09 from 'all four WDC differentials': the other three are one instrument (`import wdc_altoffset_probe as W') and belong to `wdcaltoffleg'.  Its wiringcliff exemption is re-dated naming THIS row and DELETED in the same edit as its wiring when this ships.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34992:   "id": "xscaleinten",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34996:   "title": "cpu_arm_coproc.c arm_coproc_i80321_6: reading the modelled XScale CP6 interrupt-enable register loads the correct value into rd and then calls exit(1) -- while the ADJACENT case 4 reads its own modelled register with no fatal and no exit",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34997:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1. VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file before filing.\n\nIn `arm_coproc_i80321_6', crm 0 / crn 0, the load arm reads:\n    cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\");\n    exit(1);\nThe destination register receives the modelled value and the process then dies.  The STORE arm immediately below writes the same field, and the i80321 interrupt controller consumes it, so the register is fully modelled in both directions.\n\n*** THE DECISIVE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING CASE IN THE SAME SWITCH, and it is one screen away: `case 4:' (i80321_isteer) has the IDENTICAL structure -- load arm assigns `cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer;', store arm writes it -- and carries NO fatal() and NO exit(). ***  So crn 0's kill is an OUTLIER inside its own switch rather than a policy, which is the upstream-residue pattern this project has now found several times: the author hit the exit he happened to reach while developing, and the sibling he did not.  That is a far stronger warrant than 'it is a host kill', and it is one grep away -- exactly what the option-set rule's sibling check is for.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_iq80321.c installs it explicitly (`cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = arm_coproc_i80321_6;'), and Iyonix does the same.  This is not an unattached helper. Emulated firmware reading its own interrupt-enable register is the natural consumer, and the generic coprocessor dispatcher enforces no supervisor-only precondition of its own.\n\nDISPOSITION: option-set member 4 (ACCEPT) -- delete the two lines, keep the assignment, with NO latch, for the same reason as [[mapledmaaddr]]: the read is implemented and is not ignored, so an announce would ship a wrong record.  FAULT is unavailable here in any case: memory_rw.c delivers a failed-device exception for MIPS and M88K only, and this is ARM.\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built; the rung-3 attempt is owed.  Note a witness needs a coprocessor read from an ARM guest, which is a different vehicle from the device probes this project mostly writes -- gate_arm.sh's 358 decode checks are the nearest existing machinery.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: ACCEPT IS RATIFIED, BUT MY SIBLING WARRANT WAS OVERCLAIMED AND IS NARROWED HERE.  Verified locally before recording. ***\n\nI wrote that the adjacent `case 4:' (isteer) 'carries NO fatal() and NO exit()'.  **FALSE ABOUT THE WHOLE CASE.**  Its READ arm is quiet, as I said -- but its STORE arm assigns the field and then `exit(1)' on ANY NONZERO VALUE.\n\n**THE ASYMMETRY I RELIED ON SURVIVES, AND ONLY IN THE READ DIRECTION:** case 4 READS quietly while case 0 READS and dies, which is the outlier evidence.  In the WRITE direction the two swap over -- case 0 writes quietly, case 4 writes and dies on nonzero.  So the file has kills in both cases, on opposite halves, and the correct statement is that THE READ HALVES ARE ASYMMETRIC, not that one case is clean and the other is not.\n\nThe verdict stands with a narrower warrant: **ACCEPT for the ordinary interrupt-enable read into a general register.**  Its sibling's write-side kill is a SEPARATE defect on a separate arm and belongs to a separate row, not folded into this one."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:35029:   "note": "FILED 2026-09-10.  Evidence: regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt (GPT-6 Astra at max), which enumerated the population properly for the first time.  **This row exists so the next session reads the census instead of re-running it**, which has now happened twice.\n\n*** THE EARLIER FIGURES WERE RIGHT AND THEIR DESCRIPTION WAS WRONG: 370 and 240 count `exit(1)', NOT every `exit()'. ***  Re-derived by removing comments and string literals while preserving line positions, then locating the calls, with an independent character-state lexer returning exactly the same positions:\n\n    src/devices    263 literal `exit('   135 calls after lexical filtering\n    src/cpus       112                    74\n    src/machines    35                    34\n    TOTAL          410                   243   (240 of them exit(1))\n\nLOCATION SPLIT of those 243: 150 runtime handlers and helpers, 80 init/configuration, 13 CPU generators and a standalone experiment.  The debugger is outside that scope and adds three more.\n\nAND BEING INSIDE A RUNTIME FUNCTION IS NOT REACHABILITY.  The 150 split: **98 input-reachable through enabled in-tree wiring**; 33 dead, precluded, or not established reachable; 10 optional instrumentation; 6 unattached or disabled attachments; 2 intentional halt/reboot; 1 host RAM-config check misplaced in an access handler.\n\nOWNERSHIP is an OVERLAY, not a disjoint population -- 22 live exits and 3 others are actively owned (n6 holds eight footbridge sites; alphavmkill one; sgirekills five; lcaisalane and lcabadval eight between them; m88kexit two dead ones).  **A strict open/held filter leaves 83, of which seven belong to in-work LCA repairs, so THE UNOWNED REMAINDER IS 76.**\n\n*** AND ONE OF MY OWN EARLIER QUALIFICATIONS WAS TOO AGGRESSIVE, corrected here: lacking a separate CHILD repair id does not mean lacking an open OWNING row.  `sgirekills' explicitly retains its five command exits.  Conversely an incidental mention in census prose is NOT a repair row. ***\n\nSIX E1 PREDICATES were identified -- a one-to-five-line change with an existing test carrier -- and ALL SIX ARE NOW FILED as their own rows: [[mk48nvramkill]], [[mapledmaaddr]], [[xscaleinten]], [[crimewidthkill]], [[vmedefaultkill]], [[fbctrlportkill]].  **This row therefore tracks the ~70 that remain, and they are E2/E3: real rounds, not quick wins.**  The heaviest concentrations are dev_osiop.c (14), cpu_arm_coproc.c (8 of which one is filed), dev_adb.c (7), dev_dreamcast_g2.c (6), dev_pvr.c and dev_igsfb.c (5 each).\n\nTHE ONE CONSTRAINT THAT GOVERNS EVERY DISPOSITION IN THIS BODY, and it is easy to forget: **memory_rw.c turns a failed device callback into an architectural access exception for MIPS and M88K ONLY.**  Returning zero from an ARM or SH device callback gives NO bus-error delivery, so the FAULT member is unavailable there and a round reaching for it owes the exception path first.  CPU translation and instruction handlers have their own direct mechanisms, which is a separate question."
